## 4. Main Shared Data Structures

### 4.1 Compile-time constants (globals.h)

| Constant | Value | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `NUM_WORKERS` | `BCDB_DEFAULT_WORKER_COUNT` (2) | Default worker count if GUC not set |
| `BCDB_DEFAULT_WORKER_COUNT` | 2 | Seeds `bcdb_worker_count` GUC default |
| `MAX_TX_PER_BLOCK` | 5500 | Hard cap on transactions per block and size of `txs[]` / `done_conds[]` in `BCBlock`; also the `TxQueue` partition count |
| `BCDB_RESULT_RING_CAPACITY` | 2048 | Compile-time capacity of the three result-ring arrays (`result[]`, `result_committed_txid[]`, `result_commit_xid[]`). Sized at `2 × bcdb_worker_count_max (1024)` so `bcdb_get_runtime_result_ring_slots()` never produces an out-of-bounds index. Replaces `MAX_TX_PER_BLOCK` for these arrays, saving ≈ 3.4 MB per BCBlock in shared memory. |
| `QUEUEING_BLOCKS` | 256 | Back-pressure window for block submission |
| `CLEANING_DELAY_BLOCKS` | 5 | How long (in blocks) before a committed block's shared memory is reclaimed |
| `MAX_NUM_BLOCKS` | `QUEUEING_BLOCKS + CLEANING_DELAY_BLOCKS + 100` (361) | `block_pool` hash-table capacity |
| `MAX_SHM_TX` | `(CLEANING_DELAY_BLOCKS + QUEUEING_BLOCKS + 100) * MAX_TX_PER_BLOCK` | `tx_pool` capacity (≈ 1.98 M entries) |
| `MAX_WRITE_CONFLICT` | `MAX_TX_PER_BLOCK * 128` (704 000) | Per-shard write-conflict table entry limit |
| `WRITE_CONFLICT_MAP_NUM_PARTITIONS` | 512 | Number of spinlock-sharded partitions in `ws_table` — reduces contention during `publish_ws_tableDT` |
| `HASHTAB_SWITCH_THRESHOLD` | `BCDB_DEFAULT_HASHTAB_SWITCH_THRESHOLD` (1500) | Entry count in `ws_table->map` that triggers the ping-pong epoch switch; overridable via `bcdb_dt_hashtab_switch_threshold` GUC |
| `BCDB_DEFAULT_HASHTAB_SWITCH_THRESHOLD` | 1500 | Seeds the GUC default |
| `BCDB_DEFAULT_RESULT_RING_SLOTS` | 128 | Seeds the `bcdb_result_ring_slots` GUC default |
| `BCDB_SERIAL_GATE_MODE_POLL` | 0 | Constant for poll-based serial gate (default) |
| `BCDB_SERIAL_GATE_MODE_CONDVAR` | 1 | Constant for condvar-based serial gate |
| `DT_CONFLICT_TRACKING` | 0 | Compile-time macro; runtime behavior controlled by `bcdb_dt_conflict_tracking` GUC |
| `TX_HASH_SIZE` | 32 | Bytes in the transaction hash key field (`BCDBShmXact.hash`) — only the first 8 bytes are used for the `"XXXXXXXX"` zero-padded integer format |
| `NUM_TX_QUEUE_PARTITION` | `MAX_TX_PER_BLOCK` (5500) | Total number of `TxQueue` partitions allocated; active partitions = `get_blksz()` |
| `WORKER_INIT_NUM` | 64 | Initial worker pool size hint |
| `WORK_TOKENS` | 64 | Legacy work-token concurrency control budget — **dead constant**; the `work_token` field was removed from `BlockMeta` and `holding_token` was removed from `BCDBShmXact`; `ReleaseToken`/`ForceGetToken` macros reference these removed fields and must not be called |
| `AVAILABLE_LIST_PARTITION_SIZE` | `MAX_WRITE_CONFLICT * MAX_TX_PER_BLOCK / WRITE_CONFLICT_MAP_NUM_PARTITIONS` | Per-partition available-list size for conflict tables |
| `NUM_BMIN_COND` | 1 | Number of bmin condition variables in `BlockMeta.conds[]` |
| `REPORT_INTERVAL` | 2 | Seconds between TPS progress reports |

### 4.2 BCBlock (shm_block.h)

The central shared-memory structure.  Every field matters:

```c
typedef struct {
    BCBlockID  id;
    int        num_tx;
    int volatile last_committed_tx_id     // global DT commit watermark
        pg_attribute_aligned(PG_CACHE_LINE_SIZE);
    int volatile num_ready;
    int volatile num_finished;
    BCDBShmXact *txs[MAX_TX_PER_BLOCK];
    ConditionVariable cond;               // legacy "all ready" condition
    ConditionVariable condRecovery;       // saveState/releaseState recovery
    ConditionVariable condCommit;         // condvar-mode serial gate wakeup
    int num_tx_sub;                       // submitted tx count (sentinel only)
    int num_tx_qd;                        // queued tx count (sentinel only)
    int blksize;                          // worker count (sentinel only)
    int snapTid;
    ConditionVariable done_conds[MAX_TX_PER_BLOCK];
    char result[BCDB_RESULT_RING_CAPACITY][1024];  // result ring: SELECT text / DML tag
    /* --- MUST BE LAST: see CV-layout hazard in Section 14 --- */
    int volatile published_max_tx_id;     // Lever D v2 gate counter
    int32 volatile result_committed_txid[BCDB_RESULT_RING_CAPACITY]; // T3 per-slot marker
    TransactionId volatile result_commit_xid[BCDB_RESULT_RING_CAPACITY]; // T3-v2 XID
} BCBlock;
```

**Why `last_committed_tx_id` is cache-line aligned**: every worker reads this
field in a tight spin loop in `bcdb_wait_for_prev_committed`.  Without
cache-line alignment, `last_committed_tx_id` would sit on the same cache line
as `num_tx` and the other counters, causing false sharing: every write to
`num_tx` would force all workers waiting on `last_committed_tx_id` to re-fetch
the line from the modifying CPU.  The `pg_attribute_aligned(PG_CACHE_LINE_SIZE)`
annotation puts it on its own line.

**Why `result[]` is in the sentinel block**: SELECT results from worker
optimistic runs need to survive until the middleware polls them.  The sentinel
block's result ring is the global scratchpad.  Each slot `result[tx_id % slots]`
is written by the worker that owns that tx_id.  A wrap-around race is not
possible because `slots >= 2 * num_workers`, guaranteeing that by the time slot
S wraps around to a new tx_id, the old tx_id's result has already been read.

**Result ring slot calculation example** (4 workers, `bcdb_result_ring_slots=128`):
```
runtime_slots = max(128, 2*4) = 128

tx_id=42  → slot = 42  % 128 = 42
tx_id=170 → slot = 170 % 128 = 42   (same physical slot, next wrap-around)

By the time tx_id=170 occupies slot 42, tx_id=42's result was read at
least 128 tx_ids ago — safe because all 128 workers would have to finish
first, by which point the middleware has long since read tx_id=42.

Negative index guard (test scenarios with negative tx_ids):
    idx = tx_id % slots;
    if (idx < 0) idx += slots;   // e.g. tx_id=-1: (-1%128+128) = 127
```

**The T3 arrays at the end**: `result_committed_txid[]` and
`result_commit_xid[]` exist to decouple middleware readback from the global
`last_committed_tx_id` watermark.  Previously middleware had to wait for the
entire sequence `tx_0, tx_1, ... tx_N` to commit before it could read tx_N's
result, because the only signal was the global watermark.  T3 adds per-slot
markers so middleware can learn "tx N committed and its result is in
result[slot]" as soon as that specific tx finishes, without needing any
predecessor.

**Why these fields are placed LAST**: placing new fields after `result[]` is a
hard constraint.  See Section 14 for the full explanation of the CV layout
hazard.

### 4.3 BlockMeta (shm_block.h)

A singleton in shared memory holding global bookkeeping:

```c
typedef struct {
    BCBlockID volatile global_bmin;         // oldest live block ID
    BCBlockID volatile global_bmax;         // newest submitted block ID
    ConditionVariable conds[NUM_BMIN_COND]; // bmin advancement CVs (used by WaitGlobalBmin)
    ConditionVariable token_cond;           // remnant of the removed work-token system
    uint32 debug_seq;                       // sequence counter for debug logging
    int32 volatile num_committed;           // global committed tx count (mirrors last_committed_tx_id)
    int32 volatile num_aborted;
    uint64 volatile previous_report_ts;     // timestamp of last TPS report
    int32 volatile previous_report_commit;  // commit count at last TPS report
} BlockMeta;
```

**Field notes:**

`global_bmin` / `global_bmax` define the live block window in the legacy non-DT
path; `WaitGlobalBmin(bmin)` sleeps on `conds[bmin % NUM_BMIN_COND]` until
`global_bmin` advances.  In the DT path these are unused.

`num_committed` is kept in sync with `last_committed_tx_id` by
`set_last_committed_txid()`, which writes it atomically with RELEASE ordering
immediately after advancing the watermark.  `advance_last_committed_txid()` also
contains the matching write, but that function is **not yet called** from
`worker.c` (see Section 10).

`token_cond` is a remnant of the **removed work-token concurrency control
system**.  The matching `work_token` field (an `int32` semaphore counter) and the
`holding_token` flag on `BCDBShmXact` were removed from their respective structs.
The macros `ReleaseToken()`, `ForceGetToken()`,
`WaitConditionAndReleaseToken()`, and `WaitConditionAndReleaseTokenWithBackoff()`
in `globals.h` still reference `block_meta->work_token` and
`activeTx->holding_token` — **they would not compile if called**.  They are dead
code and must not be invoked.

### 4.4 BCDBShmXact (shm_transaction.h)

The central per-transaction record in the shared-memory transaction pool.
Every in-flight transaction has exactly one of these.  Complete field table:

| Field | Type | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `hash` | `char[TX_HASH_SIZE]` | 32-byte hash key; only the first 8 bytes carry data (zero-padded decimal tx_id string, e.g. `"00000042"` for tx_id=42); remaining 24 bytes are `\0`; the fresh-server guard looks up hash `"00000000"` to detect tx_id=0 |
| `tx_id` | `BCTxID` | Assigned deterministic ordering position |
| `tx_id_committed` | `BCTxID` | Snapshot of `last_committed_txid` at execution start; refreshed on every retry; conflict-check lower bound |
| `snap_xmin` | `TransactionId` | `xmin` of the PostgreSQL snapshot taken at `GetTransactionSnapshot()` — saved for T3-v2 XID-based conflict skip |
| `ws_table` | `WSTable *` | Pointer to the shared write-set conflict table (set from `tx_pool`) |
| `rs_table` | `WSTable *` | Pointer to the shared read-set conflict table |
| `block_id_snapshot` | `BCBlockID` | Set to `block_id_committed - 1` at tx start; defines the snapshot visibility horizon |
| `block_id_committed` | `BCBlockID volatile` | The blockchain block this tx belongs to |
| `sql` | `char[1024]` | The SQL statement to execute (single statement only) |
| `status` | `TxStatus volatile` | State machine value — see full enum below |
| `queryDesc` | `QueryDesc *` | Executor state; NULLed after `FreeQueryDesc` in the outer tx body |
| `portal` | `Portal` | PostgreSQL portal holding the plan — **note: `Portal` is a typedef for `PortalData *`**, so this field IS a pointer; set during `get_write_set()`, nulled after `PortalDrop` |
| `isolation` | `int` | Isolation level written to `XactIsoLevel` at tx start |
| `pred_lock` | `bool` | When `false`, skips taking the actual SSI predicate lock (BCDB handles conflict detection itself) |
| `link` | `SIMPLEQ_ENTRY` | Intrusive SIMPLEQ link for the tx-pool free list |
| `queue_link` | `TAILQ_ENTRY` | Intrusive TAILQ link for the per-worker `TxQueue` |
| `queue_partition` | `int32` | Which `TxQueue` partition (0 .. `NUM_TX_QUEUE_PARTITION-1`) this tx was assigned to |
| `optim_write_list` | `SIMPLEQ_HEAD` | Singly-linked list of `OptimWriteEntry` records buffered during the optimistic phase; drained by `apply_optim_writes()` |
| `sxact` | `SERIALIZABLEXACT *` | SSI serializable transaction state; `sxact->bcdb_tx` back-pointer is set so SSI machinery can identify which BCDB tx owns a predicate lock |
| `cond` | `ConditionVariable` | Per-tx condition variable (used by legacy `done_conds`-style wait paths) |
| `lock` | `LWLock` | Per-tx LW lock |
| `xid` | `TransactionId` | PostgreSQL XID assigned during `start_xact_command`; stored to `result_commit_xid[slot]` for T3-v2 |
| `worker_pid` | `pid_t` | PID of the worker backend processing this tx |
| `worker` | `void *` | Opaque pointer to the `WorkerController` handle — `void *` avoids including frontend headers in this shared-memory header |
| `why_doomed` | `char[258]` | Diagnostic string populated when a tx is doomed or aborted; helps post-mortem debugging |
| `state_hash` | `SHA256_CTX` | Incremental OpenSSL SHA256 state for per-tx blockchain state hashing |
| `has_war` | `bool` | Write-after-read conflict flag set by the SSI machinery |
| `has_raw` | `bool` | Read-after-write conflict flag |
| `create_time` | `uint64` | Microsecond timestamp when the tx was created (used in non-DT timing) |
| `start_simulation_time` | `uint64` | Start of optimistic simulation phase (non-DT timing) |
| `start_parsing_time` | `uint64` | Set at entry to `get_write_set` |
| `start_checking_time` | `uint64` | Start of conflict check |
| `end_checking_time` | `uint64` | End of conflict check |
| `end_parsing_time` | `uint64` | Set after `get_write_set` returns |
| `end_simulation_time` | `uint64` | End of simulation phase |
| `start_local_copy_time` / `end_local_copy_time` | `uint64` | Legacy timing for local copy phase |
| `commit_time` | `uint64` | Microsecond timestamp of commit |

**`TxStatus` enum — full state machine:**

```c
typedef enum {
    TX_SCHEDULING,      // newly created, not yet queued
    TX_WAITING,         // in queue, waiting for a worker to pick it up
    TX_EXECUTING,       // worker has started the optimistic phase (set before start_xact_command)
    TX_WAIT_FOR_BLOCK,  // legacy non-DT: waiting for block ready signal
    TX_EXEC_WRITE_SET,  // legacy non-DT: executing write-set collection
    TX_WAIT_FOR_COMMIT, // optimistic phase done; waiting to enter serial gate
    TX_COMMITTING,      // passed serial gate; in conflict-check / publish / apply
    TX_COMMITED,        // apply done; about to write result and advance watermark (note: typo in code)
    TX_ABORTED,         // tx was aborted (terminal)
    TX_ABORTING,        // in the process of aborting
    TX_RETRYING,        // being retried after a conflict or apply failure
} TxStatus;
```

**DT path state transitions:**

```
TX_SCHEDULING → (queued) → TX_WAITING → (worker picks up) →
TX_EXECUTING → (portal run done) → TX_WAIT_FOR_COMMIT →
(gate + conflict check) → TX_COMMITTING →
(apply done) → TX_COMMITED → (cleanup, deleted from pool)

On apply retry (non-unique error after 64 retries):
    TX_COMMITTING → [full restart] → TX_EXECUTING (rw_conflicts=1, continue)

On unique-violation exhaustion:
    TX_COMMITTING → TX_COMMITED with apply_terminal_noop=true (affected_rows=0)

On PG_CATCH (exception):
    Any state → delete_tx → activeTx=NULL → PG_RE_THROW
```

`activeTx` is a process-local pointer (declared in `shm_transaction.c`)
pointing to the `BCDBShmXact` currently being processed by this worker.  It is
set at the top of `bcdb_worker_process_tx_dt()` and cleared on completion.
Since it is process-local (not shared across workers), it never needs a lock.

### 4.5 TxQueue

`create_tx_pool()` (called from `ipci.c` at postmaster startup) allocates
`NUM_TX_QUEUE_PARTITION` (= `MAX_TX_PER_BLOCK` = 5500) `TxQueue` objects in
shared memory.  Each partition holds:

```c
typedef struct _TxQueue {
    TAILQ_HEAD(_TxAttachedQueueHead, _BCDBShmXact) list;
    slock_t            lock;        // serializes enqueue/dequeue
    ConditionVariable  empty_cond;  // CONSUMER (worker) blocks here when queue is empty
    ConditionVariable  full_cond;   // PRODUCER (middleware) blocks here when queue is too full
    int32 volatile     size;
} TxQueue;
```

**Critical naming note — `empty_cond` vs `full_cond`:**

| CV | Who waits | Condition | Who signals |
|---|---|---|---|
| `empty_cond` | **Consumer** (worker, `tx_queue_next`) | queue `size == 0` | Producer, after enqueue |
| `full_cond` | **Producer** (middleware, `tx_queue_insert`) | queue `size > QUEUEING_BLOCKS` | Consumer, after dequeue |

The active queue count equals `get_blksz()` (the configured worker count);
only partitions `0 .. get_blksz()-1` are used.  Each transaction is assigned
to partition `tx_id % get_blksz()`.  This ensures workers 0..N-1 each receive
a balanced slice of the tx_id space, and within each worker's slice,
transactions arrive in tx_id order.

**Queue partition assignment example** (4 workers, `blksize=4`):
```
tx_id=0  → partition 0 % 4 = 0  → worker 0
tx_id=1  → partition 1 % 4 = 1  → worker 1
tx_id=2  → partition 2 % 4 = 2  → worker 2
tx_id=3  → partition 3 % 4 = 3  → worker 3
tx_id=4  → partition 4 % 4 = 0  → worker 0 again
tx_id=5  → partition 5 % 4 = 1  → worker 1 again

Worker 0's queue receives: tx_ids 0, 4, 8, 12, 16, ...  (in order within that queue)
Worker 1's queue receives: tx_ids 1, 5, 9, 13, 17, ...
```

**Back-pressure example:** with 8 workers and `QUEUEING_BLOCKS=256`, each
queue holds up to 256 entries before the producer blocks on `full_cond`.  So
the maximum in-flight tx count before back-pressure kicks in is
`8 * 256 = 2048`.

### 4.6 Conflict tables (WSTable)

The write-set conflict detection tables.  `create_tx_pool()` allocates:

**ws_table** (write-set table):
- `map` — one of the two alternating write-set hash tables (keyed by `PREDICATELOCKTARGETTAG`); active during even epochs (`tx_id / threshold` is even)
- `mapB` — the other alternating hash table; active during odd epochs (`tx_id / threshold` is odd)
- `mapActive` — pointer to whichever of `map`/`mapB` is the active publish target for the current tx_id's epoch; re-derived on every call to `publish_ws_tableDT`
- `WRITE_CONFLICT_MAP_NUM_PARTITIONS` spinlocks (one per hash partition, avoiding a single global lock)
- `mapB_nonempty` — `pg_atomic_uint32` flag; set to 1 when any entry is published to `mapB` during an odd epoch, reset to 0 only at the start of the next odd epoch (when `mapB` is cleared).  This allows `table_checkDT` to skip the `mapB` probe entirely with a single atomic load when `mapB` is empty.

**rs_table** (read-set table):
- Only `map` is initialized.  `rs_table_check()` queries this.
- In the current runtime (`bcdb_dt_conflict_tracking=off`), this table is never populated.

**Why two maps (ping-pong)?**  As transactions commit across many blocks, the
active hash table accumulates write-set entries.  Instead of clearing a single
map at a fixed threshold (which would lose data still needed for conflict
checks), `publish_ws_tableDT()` alternates between `map` and `mapB` on an
epoch schedule driven by `tx_id`:

```
epoch = tx_id / HASHTAB_SWITCH_THRESHOLD   (integer division)
```

- **Even epoch** (`epoch % 2 == 0`): `mapActive = map`.  At epoch start
  (`tx_id % threshold == 0`), `map` is cleared to reclaim space.
- **Odd epoch** (`epoch % 2 != 0`): `mapActive = mapB`.  At epoch start,
  `mapB` is cleared and `mapB_nonempty` is reset to 0.

At any epoch boundary the *newly-active* shard is cleared (not the old one),
so the previous epoch's entries remain in the other shard and are still visible
to conflict checks until that shard becomes active again.  `conflict_checkDT()`
always probes `map`; it also probes `mapB` when `mapB_nonempty != 0`.

**Example epoch boundaries (threshold=1500):**
```
tx_ids   0 – 1499  → epoch 0 (even):  mapActive = map;   map cleared at tx_id=0
tx_ids 1500 – 2999 → epoch 1 (odd):   mapActive = mapB;  mapB cleared at tx_id=1500,
                                        mapB_nonempty reset to 0
tx_ids 3000 – 4499 → epoch 2 (even):  mapActive = map;   map cleared at tx_id=3000
tx_ids 4500 – 5999 → epoch 3 (odd):   mapActive = mapB;  mapB cleared at tx_id=4500

During epoch 1 (tx_ids 1500–2999):
    publish_ws_tableDT writes into mapB → sets mapB_nonempty=1
    conflict_checkDT probes map (has epoch-0 entries) AND mapB (current epoch)
During epoch 2 (tx_ids 3000–4499):
    publish_ws_tableDT writes into map (cleared at epoch start)
    mapB_nonempty still 1 (not yet reset) → conflict_checkDT probes both
    map (current) and mapB (epoch-1 entries) until mapB_nonempty is reset at
    epoch 3 start (tx_id=4500)
```

### 4.7 Supporting structures in shm_transaction.h

**`OptimWriteEntry`** — one buffered DML operation:

```c
typedef struct _OptimWriteEntry {
    CmdType         operation;      // CMD_INSERT, CMD_UPDATE, CMD_DELETE
    TupleTableSlot  *slot;          // new tuple data (INSERT/UPDATE); NULL for DELETE
    ItemPointerData  old_tid;       // old TID for UPDATE/DELETE
    Oid              relOid;        // relation OID (needed for CMD_DELETE)
    int32            keyval;        // primary-key value for deferred DELETE-by-key
    CommandId        cid;
    SIMPLEQ_ENTRY(_OptimWriteEntry) link;
} OptimWriteEntry;
```

Each entry appended to `tx->optim_write_list` during the optimistic phase is
applied during the serial phase by `apply_optim_writes()`.

**`XidMapEntry`** — XID → tx mapping:

```c
typedef struct _XidMapEntry {
    TransactionId    xid;   // PostgreSQL TransactionId (key)
    BCDBShmXact     *tx;    // pointer to the in-flight tx
} XidMapEntry;
```

Populated by `add_tx_xid_map(xid, tx)` (called after `start_xact_command`
assigns the XID) and cleared by `remove_tx_xid_map(xid)` on commit or abort.
Allows SSI callbacks and cleanup paths to look up the BCDB tx from a bare
PostgreSQL XID.

**`WSTableEntry`** / **`WSTableEntryRecord`**:

```c
typedef struct _WSTableEntry {
    PREDICATELOCKTARGETTAG  tag;    // shared hash-table key (global conflict map)
    BCTxID                  tx_id;  // latest tx_id that wrote this tag
} WSTableEntry;

typedef struct _WSTableEntryRecord {
    PREDICATELOCKTARGETTAG  tag;    // process-local list entry (not in shared map yet)
    LIST_ENTRY(_WSTableEntryRecord) link;
} WSTableEntryRecord;
```

`WSTableEntryRecord` is the process-local pending list entry accumulated
during the optimistic phase (`ws_table_reserveDT`).  `WSTableEntry` is the
shared-map entry written during `publish_ws_tableDT`.

**`PendingMerkleUpdate`** / **`MerkleChangeSet`** — batched Merkle updates:

```c
typedef struct _PendingMerkleUpdate {
    Oid         indexOid;       // Merkle index to update
    int         partitionId;    // partition within the index
    MerkleHash  hash;           // the hash value to XOR in or out
    bool        is_insert;      // true = XOR in (new row), false = XOR out (old row)
    LIST_ENTRY(_PendingMerkleUpdate) link;
} PendingMerkleUpdate;

typedef LIST_HEAD(_MerkleChangeSet, _PendingMerkleUpdate) MerkleChangeSet;
```

During `apply_optim_writes()`, each heap mutation records a `PendingMerkleUpdate`
via `merkle_record_update()`.  After all writes succeed,
`apply_merkle_changeset()` applies the full batch atomically as XOR
operations on the Merkle index.  Batching avoids partial Merkle updates when
apply is retried inside a rolled-back subtransaction.

---

## 5. PostgreSQL Integration Points

BCDB is deeply embedded in the PostgreSQL engine.  Understanding these hook
points is essential for understanding where each phase of a transaction happens.

### 5.1 Shared-memory initialization (ipci.c)

During the postmaster's shared-memory sizing/init pass, `ipci.c` calls:

- `create_tx_pool()` — allocates the transaction hash tables, queue partitions, and conflict tables
- `create_block_pool()` — allocates `BlockMeta`, `block_pool_lock`, and the `block_pool` hash table

This happens exactly once, before any worker forks, so all workers see a
consistent initial state.

**Important**: `create_block_pool()` does **not** call `set_blksz(1)` for block
`1`.  The sentinel block does not exist yet at that point (the hash table is
initialized but empty).  Block `1` is created lazily on first access via
`get_block_by_id(1, true)`, at which point `blksize` is initialized from
`bcdb_worker_count`.

### 5.2 Worker startup (postgres.c)

When `postgres.c` starts a backend with `is_bcdb_worker=true`, it calls
`bcdb_worker_init()` to establish the worker's private memory contexts.  Then
when it receives the `'J'` protocol message, it enters the dequeue loop.

There is also a "direct execution" path in `postgres.c` that recognizes queries
prefixed with `s <8-digit-id> ...` (the hash prefix format).  This path creates
a `BCDBShmXact` directly and calls `safedb_txdt(tx, true)`, which wraps
`bcdb_worker_process_tx_dt` with `is_bcdb_worker` save/restore logic.  This
path is used for testing/development; the production path uses the `'J'` queue.

`postgres.c` contains a complete EVP-based signing infrastructure used inside
the simple-query (`'Q'`) handling path (not the `'J'` worker dequeue loop).

**Core helpers:**

- `base64_decode(const char *input, int length, int *out_len)` — OpenSSL BIO
  base64 decoder; used to decode both the DER public key and the per-call
  signature blob.
- `verify_signature_b64key(const char *public_key_b64, const char *message, const char *signature_b64)`
  — Takes a **Base64-encoded DER SubjectPublicKeyInfo** key (loaded with
  `d2i_PUBKEY()`), decodes the signature, and calls `EVP_DigestVerifyInit` /
  `EVP_DigestVerifyUpdate` / `EVP_DigestVerifyFinal` with `EVP_sha256()`.
  **This key format is DER, not PEM** — unlike `bcdb_verify()` in `func.c`
  which accepts a PEM-formatted key via `PEM_read_bio_RSA_PUBKEY()`.

**Process-local cache:**

To avoid re-decoding and re-parsing the public key on every transaction,
`verify_signature_b64key` maintains three static process-local variables:

```c
static EVP_PKEY   *bcdb_cached_pubkey    = NULL;      // parsed DER key object
static char        bcdb_cached_pubkey_b64[2048] = ""; // base64 of the current key
static EVP_MD_CTX *bcdb_cached_md_ctx    = NULL;      // reused digest context
```

On each call, if `public_key_b64` matches `bcdb_cached_pubkey_b64` the parse
step is skipped entirely; only the per-call base64 signature decode and the
three-step EVP digest verify are charged.  The signature is always decoded
fresh because it is unique per transaction.

**Performance telemetry:**

```c
typedef struct BcdbSigVerifyMetrics {
    uint64  calls;
    uint64  success;
    uint64  failure;
    double  total_us;
    double  decode_pub_us;   // charged only on key cache miss
    double  decode_sig_us;   // charged per call
    double  load_pub_us;     // charged only on key cache miss
    double  digest_init_us;
    double  digest_update_us;
    double  digest_final_us;
} BcdbSigVerifyMetrics;
```

`bcdb_log_signature_verify_summary()` emits a `LOG` line with all counters.
It fires automatically every 5 000 calls and once more at process exit (via the
`on_proc_exit` hook registered in `PostgresMain`).

**Key lookup helper:**

`bcdb_client_public_key_or_default()` returns the GUC value
`bcdb_client_public_key` if it is non-empty; otherwise it returns a hardcoded
Base64-DER development key (`publicKey2` in earlier code comments).  This means
the signing path always has a usable key even without explicit configuration.

**Failure handling:**

`bcdb_handle_signature_failure(query_string, valid)` is called when
`verify_signature_b64key` returns 0:

- `bcdb_enforce_signatures = off` → `ereport(WARNING, ...)` and continue
- `bcdb_enforce_signatures = on` → `ereport(ERROR, ...)` and reject the statement

**Where the signing check is inserted in the `'Q'` path:**

The query string is scanned for a `##` two-character delimiter.  When found:
- The bytes before `##` are the signature (Base64-encoded).
- The bytes after `##` are the actual SQL (`query_string2`).
- `verify_signature_b64key(publicKey2, query_string2, qsig)` is called.
- On success (`valid == 1`) the undecorated SQL is dispatched.

This logic is reached in both:

- the direct hashed deterministic path (`s <8-digit-id> ...`) before
  `create_tx(...)/safedb_txdt(...)`
- the regular simple-query path before `exec_simple_query(...)`

**Key distinctions from `bcdb_verify()` in `src/backend/bcdb/func.c`:**

| | `postgres.c` inline path | `func.c` `bcdb_verify()` |
|---|---|---|
| Key format | Base64-encoded DER (`d2i_PUBKEY`) | PEM (`PEM_read_bio_RSA_PUBKEY`) |
| EVP API | `EVP_PKEY` + `EVP_DigestVerify*` | `RSA *` + `EVP_PKEY_assign_RSA` |
| Caching | Process-local key + ctx cache | None |
| Telemetry | `BcdbSigVerifyMetrics` | None |
| Invocation | Automatic on every signed `'Q'` query | Explicit SQL call by client |

For the direct path, `set_blksz(1)` is called only if `get_blksz() <= 0`,
specifically to avoid clobbering an already-configured runtime `blksize`.  This
guard was added after discovering that a fresh-server direct-exec run would
reset the blksize to 1 even when workers had already set it to their count.

### 5.3 DML interception (nodeModifyTable.c)

Every INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE executed by a BCDB worker goes through modified
executor paths:

**INSERT**:
1. Computes a primary-key-based `PREDICATELOCKTARGETTAG` via `bcdb_compute_key_tag(keyval, relOid)`
2. Calls `ws_table_reserveDT(&tag)` — appends a `WSTableEntryRecord` to process-local `ws_table_record`
3. Calls `store_optim_insert(slot, ...)` — appends an `OptimWriteEntry` (CMD_INSERT) to `tx->optim_write_list`

**UPDATE**:
1. Computes both a TID-based tag (covering the old tuple's heap location) and a key-based tag (covering the logical key) — two reservation points to cover the old physical location and the logical key
2. Calls `ws_table_reserveDT` for each tag
3. Calls `store_optim_update(slot, old_tid)` — buffers the new tuple slot and old TID

**DELETE**:
1. Computes both tags (same two-tag logic as UPDATE)
2. Calls `ws_table_reserveDT` for each
3. Captures the current TID and tuple slot for later serial delete
4. Calls `store_optim_delete(relOid, tupleid, slot)`

**DELETE-0 (deferred delete-by-key)**:
When a DELETE affects 0 rows (the row doesn't exist yet because a concurrent
lower-tx_id INSERT hasn't landed in the heap), BCDB detects this in
`bcdb_maybe_enqueue_deferred_delete0_by_key` (called in `worker.c` after
`get_write_set` returns, not inside `nodeModifyTable.c`).  See Section 16 for
the full description.

None of these actually modify heap pages.  The `OptimWriteEntry` records are
accumulated in `tx->optim_write_list` (a `SIMPLEQ_HEAD`) and applied only
during the serial phase by `apply_optim_writes()`.

This deferred-write design is fundamental to determinism: every worker runs its
SQL on a fixed snapshot, computes what writes *should* happen, then the serial
phase applies them in strict `tx_id` order — so the final heap state is
independent of which worker ran which transaction first.

**Edge case — two-tag UPDATE example:**

```
tx_id=10: UPDATE t SET val=99 WHERE key=5
    old TID = (page=3, offset=7)
    key=5 → hash → tag_key = {rel=1234, block=0x0002, offset=0x0001}

ws_table_reserveDT(&tag_tid)  // covers old heap location
ws_table_reserveDT(&tag_key)  // covers logical key

If tx_id=9 had an INSERT key=5 with tag_key already published,
conflict_checkDT finds tag_key in ws_table → conflict → tx_id=10 retries.
```

### 5.4 Read-set capture (predicate.c)

BCDB hooks into `PredicateLockAcquire()` to intercept SSI predicate locks:

```c
// In predicate.c, inside PredicateLockAcquire:
if (is_bcdb_worker)
    rs_table_reserveDT(targettag);
```

With `bcdb_dt_conflict_tracking=off` (the default), `rs_table_reserveDT()`
returns immediately.  With it on, it allocates a `WSTableEntryRecord` in
`bcdb_tx_context` and prepends it to `rs_table_record`.

When `activeTx->pred_lock` is false, BCDB also skips taking the actual SSI
predicate lock in PostgreSQL's lock table — because BCDB handles conflict
detection itself and the SSI overhead would be redundant.

### 5.5 SELECT result capture (printtup.c)

When a worker executes a SELECT during `PortalRun()`, the `printtup.c`
destination receiver writes formatted row text into:

```c
block->result[tx_id % bcdb_get_runtime_result_ring_slots()]
```

This is done using the same slot-sizing formula as the worker's commit path,
ensuring the result written during the optimistic phase lands in the same slot
that will be read after the serial gate.

After the serial gate (in `bcdb_worker_process_tx_dt`), the worker does:

```c
int read_idx = bcdb_result_slot_for_txid(tx->tx_id);
strlcpy(tx_result, block->result[read_idx], sizeof(tx_result));
```

This copies the SELECT result into the local `tx_result[1024]` buffer before
the conflict check and commit path.  Later, `block->result[mem_txid]` is
written with the final result string (either `tx_result` for SELECT, or
`*hash* INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE 0 N` for DML).

**Result string format examples:**
```
SELECT val FROM t WHERE id=1
    → result[slot] = "42\n"   (raw printtup output, one row per line)

INSERT INTO t VALUES(1,'foo')  (tx hash "00000003")
    → result[slot] = "\n\t*00000003* INSERT 0 1\n"

UPDATE t SET v=2 WHERE id=1    (tx hash "00000007")
    → result[slot] = "\n\t*00000007* UPDATE 0 1\n"

DELETE FROM t WHERE id=1       (tx hash "00000005")
    → result[slot] = "\n\t*00000005* DELETE 0 1\n"

Terminal noop (unique violation exhausted, tx hash "00000009")
    → result[slot] = "\n\t*00000009* INSERT 0 0\n"   (affected_rows=0)
```

### 5.6 SQL-callable functions (func.c / pg_proc.dat)

Active SQL entry points:

| Function | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `bcdb_init(bool, int4)` | Initialize BCDB subsystem |
| `bcdb_init2(bool, int4, int4, int4)` | Initialize with burst-mode parameters `(is_oep_mode, block_size, numTxBurst, burstTime)` — configures `bcdb_middleware_init2` |
| `bcdb_tx_submit(cstring)` | Submit a single transaction |
| `bcdb_add_tx_with_block_id(cstring, int4)` | Submit tx to specific block |
| `bcdb_allow_txs_commit_by_block_id(int4)` | Release commit gate for block |
| `bcdb_check_txs_result(cstring)` | Check result for specific tx hash |
| `bcdb_reset()` | Reset state |
| `bcdb_block_submit(cstring)` | Submit full JSON block |
| `bcdb_block_submit_results(cstring)` | Submit block and wait for results |
| `bcdb_wait_to_finish()` | Block until all submitted txs commit |
| `bcdb_num_committed()` | Return current committed count |
| `bcdb_last_committed_txid()` | Return `last_committed_tx_id` |
| `bcdb_verify(text, text, text)` | Verify RSA signature for `(publicKey, plainText, signature)` — **`publicKey` must be PEM-formatted** (uses `PEM_read_bio_RSA_PUBKEY`); this is distinct from the `postgres.c` inline path which takes a Base64-DER key via `d2i_PUBKEY` |
| `bcdb_check_block_status()` | Log per-block status (only functional when compiled with `#ifdef LOG_STATUS`); no-op otherwise |

### 5.7 Middleware result readback

`bcdb_middleware_submit_block_results()` (in middleware.c) submits a block
and collects results in two phases:

1. **Enqueue all txs**: iterates `block->txs[0..num_tx-1]`, calling
   `tx_queue_insert(tx, tx->tx_id)` for each, recording the last `tx_id`.
2. **Wait for the last tx**: calls `bcdb_wait_until_committed(last_tx_id)` —
   one wait on the global `last_committed_tx_id` watermark.

**Why `bcdb_wait_until_committed` (not `bcdb_wait_until_slot_ready`):**

`result_committed_txid[slot]` is set at **Step 10** (immediately after writing
the result string to the ring), which runs **before**
`bcdb_wait_for_prev_committed` (Step 11).  Because apply phases overlap (Lever D
v2), there is no ordering guarantee between per-slot markers across workers:
a fast tx_13 can fire its slot before a slow tx_10 has written its slot.

Waiting on the per-slot marker of the last tx (`bcdb_wait_until_slot_ready`) is
therefore NOT safe for reading the whole block — earlier slots may be
unpopulated when the last slot fires.

**Why `bcdb_wait_until_committed` is safe:**

- `last_committed_tx_id` advances in strict serial order at Step 11
  (`bcdb_wait_for_prev_committed` + `set_last_committed_txid`).
- Step 10 (per-slot write) always precedes Step 11 for each tx.
- Therefore: `last_committed_tx_id ≥ N`  ⟹  every slot for tx_id 0..N is
  already written and stable.

One wait on the global watermark is sufficient and race-free.

After the wait, the function reads all slots sequentially and assembles
the result string.

---

## 6. Full Transaction Lifecycle — DT Path

This section traces a single transaction through every step of
`bcdb_worker_process_tx_dt()`, explaining the reasoning at each phase.

### Step 0: Worker setup

```c
is_bcdb_worker = true;
activeTx = tx;
tx->block_id_snapshot = tx->block_id_committed - 1;
LIST_INIT(&ws_table_record);
LIST_INIT(&rs_table_record);
```

Setting `is_bcdb_worker = true` is critical: it gates all the DML intercept
logic in `nodeModifyTable.c` and the predicate lock hook in `predicate.c`.
Without this flag, the worker would apply heap changes during the optimistic
phase instead of buffering them.

`block_id_snapshot = block_id_committed - 1` defines the snapshot horizon:
the worker will see the state of all blocks before its own block.

### Step 1: Transaction retry loop

The outer `for (;;)` loop handles the case where `conflict_checkDT()` returns
1 (conflict detected) and the transaction must re-execute.  On first entry,
`init=true`; on retries, the previous attempt's state must be cleaned up:

```c
bcdb_drain_optim_write_list(activeTx);   // free SIMPLEQ entries
MemoryContextReset(bcdb_tx_context);     // reclaim all palloc'd memory
AbortCurrentTransaction();               // release buffer pins, locks
reset_xact_command();                    // set xact_started=false
activeTx->portal = NULL;                 // portal was dropped by Abort
tx->queryDesc = NULL;
tx->sxact = NULL;
```

The reason for `AbortCurrentTransaction()` (not `CommitTransaction()`) is that
after a conflict the work done in the optimistic phase must be discarded.  The
PostgreSQL transaction infrastructure releases all buffer pins, lock table
entries, and predicate lock references.  After this, `activeTx` pointers like
`portal` are stale — they must be nulled immediately.

**A critical correctness fix**: on every entry to the restart loop body
(including retries), the code does:

```c
activeTx->tx_id_committed = get_last_committed_txid(tx);
```

This refreshes the baseline of "what was committed when we took our snapshot."
Without this refresh, on a retry `conflict_checkDT()` would compare against an
outdated `tx_id_committed`, and any tx that committed *between* the first
attempt and the retry would re-appear as a conflict, causing potential livelock.

### Step 2: Start transaction, take snapshot

```c
XactIsoLevel = tx->isolation;
start_xact_command();
snapshot = GetTransactionSnapshot();
activeTx->snap_xmin = snapshot->xmin;  /* T3-v2: save for XID-based conflict skip */
tx->sxact = ShareSerializableXact();
tx->sxact->bcdb_tx = tx;
```

`start_xact_command()` begins a PostgreSQL transaction.  `GetTransactionSnapshot()`
returns a snapshot consistent with the current database state.  This snapshot
is used throughout the optimistic phase.

`snap_xmin` is saved here for T3-v2 (see Section 9).  The idea: any tx that
committed its PostgreSQL XID before our `xmin` is already visible in this
snapshot, so if a conflict probe finds one of those txs in the write-set table,
we can skip it — our snapshot already includes those writes.

`ShareSerializableXact()` registers an SSI serializable transaction.  The
`bcdb_tx` backpointer on `sxact` allows the SSI machinery to identify which
BCDB transaction a predicate lock belongs to.

### Step 3: Optimistic execution (get_write_set + post-return cleanup)

**Inside `get_write_set(tx, snapshot)`** (runs the SQL through the full pipeline):

1. `pg_parse_query()` — parse the SQL string into a `RawStmt`
2. `pg_analyze_and_rewrite()` — semantic analysis and rewrite rules
3. `pg_plan_queries()` — produce a `PlannedStmt`
4. `CreatePortal("", true, true)` — create an unnamed portal (`portal->visible = false`)
5. `PortalDefineQuery()` — attach the plan and query string
6. `PortalStart(portal, NULL, 0, snapshot)` — bind the fixed snapshot to the portal
7. `PortalRun(portal, FETCH_ALL, true, true, receiver, receiver, completionTag)` — execute

During `PortalRun()`, DML hits `nodeModifyTable.c` hooks which buffer writes.
SELECT rows are captured by `printtup.c` into `block->result[slot]`.
`get_write_set` returns after `PortalRun` completes.  The portal is still alive
at this point; `activeTx->portal` is set to it just before `PortalRun`.

**Back in `bcdb_worker_process_tx_dt`, immediately after `get_write_set` returns:**

```c
// 1. Handle DELETE-0 edge case (must happen before executor cleanup)
bcdb_maybe_enqueue_deferred_delete0_by_key(tx);

// 2. Executor cleanup — NOT inside get_write_set
ResourceOwner saved_owner = CurrentResourceOwner;
CurrentResourceOwner = activeTx->portal->resowner;
if (tx->queryDesc != NULL) {
    ExecutorFinish(tx->queryDesc);   // close cursors, flush remaining output
    ExecutorEnd(tx->queryDesc);      // release executor-level resources
    FreeQueryDesc(tx->queryDesc);    // free query plan memory
    tx->queryDesc = NULL;            // prevent double-free
}
CurrentResourceOwner = saved_owner;

hold_portal_snapshot = true;  // portal is still alive; we own its resource cleanup
tx->status = TX_WAIT_FOR_COMMIT;
```

**Why executor cleanup happens outside `get_write_set`**: the resource-owner
save/restore pattern (`CurrentResourceOwner = portal->resowner` ... restore)
must bracket `ExecutorFinish/End` to ensure PostgreSQL's resource tracking
attributes buffer pins and locks to the portal's owner.  Placing this in the
outer function keeps `get_write_set` focused purely on parse/plan/run, and lets
`bcdb_maybe_enqueue_deferred_delete0_by_key` inspect the `completionTag` before
the executor state is torn down.

`hold_portal_snapshot = true` records that the portal is still alive at this
point — its resource owner holds buffer pins and TupleDesc references that must
be explicitly released via the 3-phase `ResourceOwnerRelease` sequence in Step 9.

`tx->status = TX_WAIT_FOR_COMMIT` signals the transaction is done with its
optimistic phase and waiting for the serial slot.

### Step 4: Serial gate (`bcdb_wait_for_serial_slot`)

```c
block = get_block_by_id(1, false);
bcdb_wait_for_serial_slot(tx, block);
```

This is the **core of Lever D v2**.  The gate waits until:

```
published_max_tx_id + 1 >= tx->tx_id
```

This means: "my predecessor (tx_id - 1) has already passed through its own
conflict check AND published its write-set AND advanced `published_max`."
Once this condition holds, it is safe for me to run my conflict check, because
any tx with tx_id < mine has already registered its writes.

This is a fundamental improvement over the old gate condition
`last_committed_tx_id + 1 == my_tx_id`, which required the predecessor to
fully finish apply and `finish_xact_command` — a much heavier barrier that
forced strict serialization of apply.  Lever D v2 allows tx N and tx N+1 to
overlap in their apply/finish phases.

**Gate state during normal operation (example with 4 workers):**
```
Time → (each row is one worker, columns are tx_ids being processed)

Worker 0 (tx_id=0): [optimistic run]--[GATE: published_max=-1, wait for -1 ≥ -1 → IMMEDIATE]--[publish]--[advance max to 0]--[apply]
Worker 1 (tx_id=1):                   [optimistic run]--[GATE: wait for published_max ≥ 0]-----↑ unblocked here
Worker 2 (tx_id=2):                                                                                          [GATE: wait for published_max ≥ 1]
...

published_max starts at -1 (block init).
tx_id=0: gate condition is published_max+1 >= 0 → (-1)+1=0 >= 0 → TRUE → passes immediately.
tx_id=1: waits until published_max >= 0, which happens when tx_0 calls set_published_max_txid.
tx_id=2: waits until published_max >= 1 (i.e., tx_1 has published).
```

**After the gate**, the worker reads the result ring slot:

```c
int read_idx = bcdb_result_slot_for_txid(tx->tx_id);
strlcpy(tx_result, block->result[read_idx], sizeof(tx_result));
```

This is done **before** the conflict check.  Why?  The conflict check may
trigger a full transaction restart (loop to Step 1), which calls
`MemoryContextReset(bcdb_tx_context)`.  The `tx_result` local variable survives
this reset because it lives on the stack.  If the result were read after a
potential restart, the slot might have been overwritten by a different run.

### Step 5: Conflict check (`conflict_checkDT`)

```c
rw_conflicts = conflict_checkDT();
```

`conflict_checkDT()` iterates over `ws_table_record` (local write-set entries
buffered during the optimistic phase) and for each entry calls
`ws_table_checkDT()` -> `table_checkDT()`.  It also iterates over
`rs_table_record` and checks those against the global write-set table.

With `bcdb_dt_conflict_tracking=off` (the default runtime setting), both lists
are empty (reservations were no-ops), so `conflict_checkDT()` always returns 0
(no conflict).  This means all determinism comes from strict serial ordering
alone — transactions commit in tx_id order, so the final state is deterministic
even without explicit conflict detection.

If `rw_conflicts == 1`, the loop continues back to Step 1 (retry).
If `rw_conflicts == 0`, execution proceeds to the commit path.

### Step 6: Publish write-set

```c
tx->status = TX_COMMITTING;
publish_ws_tableDT(tx->tx_id);
```

`publish_ws_tableDT()` inserts all entries in `ws_table_record` into
`ws_table->mapActive`.  Other workers in their conflict-check phase will find
these entries.  On every call, the function re-derives the active map from the
tx_id's epoch (`epoch = tx_id / threshold`; even → `map`, odd → `mapB`) and,
at each epoch boundary (`tx_id % threshold == 0`), clears the newly-active
shard and resets `mapB_nonempty` when entering an odd epoch.  See Section 4.6
for the full epoch-parity mechanism.

### Step 7: Lever D v2 gate release

```c
set_published_max_txid(tx);
```

Immediately after publishing, tx N advances `published_max_tx_id` to `tx_id`.
This is what unblocks tx N+1 from its spin in `bcdb_wait_for_serial_slot`.

**This call is intentionally placed AFTER `publish_ws_tableDT`**.  The ordering
guarantee is: by the time tx N+1 passes the gate and runs its conflict check,
tx N's write-set is already in the conflict tables.  If `set_published_max_txid`
were called before `publish_ws_tableDT`, tx N+1 could check the tables before
tx N's entries were present, producing false negatives.

**Why CAS-max is necessary — out-of-order publish race:**
```
Scenario: tx_id=3 conflicts and is in a full restart (re-running optimistic phase).
Meanwhile, tx_id=4 passes the gate (published_max was already ≥ 3 from an earlier run)
and completes publish first.

Without CAS-max (simple store):
    tx_id=4: published_max = 4    (store)
    tx_id=3 (retry): published_max = 3  (store) ← rolls back! tx_id=5 now hangs
    tx_id=5 is waiting for published_max ≥ 4 — it just went backwards.

With CAS-max loop:
    tx_id=4: CAS(published_max, 3, 4) → succeeds → published_max = 4
    tx_id=3 (retry): CAS(published_max, 2, 3) → FAILS (current=4 ≠ 2) → loop exits
                     tx_id=3 checks: current(4) >= new_val(3) → skip, no rollback
    published_max remains 4; tx_id=5 unblocks correctly.
```

**The removed pre-apply wait**: In an earlier revision (v2-fix1), there was a
`bcdb_wait_for_prev_committed()` call between `set_published_max_txid` and
`bcdb_apply_optim_writes_with_retry`.  This was added to prevent co-write lock
contention in the apply path.  However, it was found to re-serialize the apply
phase and caused a severe regression: **8-thread TPS dropped from 335 to 223**.
The call was removed.  The comment in the code explains:

> The pre-apply bcdb_wait_for_prev_committed that was here in v2-fix1 has been
> removed — it re-serialized apply and caused the 8t 335->223 TPS regression.
> Conflict check + wait=true in apply_optim_update are sufficient to handle any
> co-write edge cases.

### Step 8: Apply buffered writes (`bcdb_apply_optim_writes_with_retry`)

```c
if (!bcdb_apply_optim_writes_with_retry(tx, &apply_retries, &apply_nonretryable))
{
    if (apply_nonretryable) {
        apply_terminal_noop = true;  // write affected_rows=0 result
    } else {
        rw_conflicts = 1;
        continue;  // full restart
    }
}
```

See Section 11 for the full apply retry design.

### Step 9: Commit and result formatting

Portal cleanup (3-phase ResourceOwnerRelease):

```c
if (hold_portal_snapshot) {
    ResourceOwnerRelease(activeTx->portal->resowner,
                         RESOURCE_RELEASE_BEFORE_LOCKS, false, false);
    ResourceOwnerRelease(activeTx->portal->resowner,
                         RESOURCE_RELEASE_LOCKS, false, false);
    ResourceOwnerRelease(activeTx->portal->resowner,
                         RESOURCE_RELEASE_AFTER_LOCKS, false, false);
    ResourceOwnerDelete(activeTx->portal->resowner);
    activeTx->portal->resowner = NULL;
    PortalDrop(activeTx->portal, false);
    activeTx->portal = NULL;
}
```

The 3-phase `ResourceOwnerRelease` (BEFORE_LOCKS -> LOCKS -> AFTER_LOCKS) is
required to release buffer pins, lock table entries, and tuple descriptor
references held by the portal's resource owner.  Without this explicit cleanup,
PostgreSQL would log "resource owner leak" warnings.

Then the `TopTransactionResourceOwner` is also explicitly released with the
**critical** `isTopLevel=true`:

```c
ResourceOwnerRelease(TopTransactionResourceOwner,
                     RESOURCE_RELEASE_BEFORE_LOCKS, false, true);
ResourceOwnerRelease(TopTransactionResourceOwner,
                     RESOURCE_RELEASE_LOCKS, false, true);
ResourceOwnerRelease(TopTransactionResourceOwner,
                     RESOURCE_RELEASE_AFTER_LOCKS, false, true);
finish_xact_command();
```

**Why `isTopLevel=true` is critical**: PostgreSQL's `ResourceOwnerRelease`
has an assertion at `resowner.c:582` that fires if `isTopLevel=false` is passed
for a resource owner that has no parent (i.e., `TopTransactionResourceOwner`).
When `isTopLevel=false` the code assumes it is releasing a *sub*transaction
owner and tries to do retail lock release expecting a parent; finding no parent
panics.  Standard PostgreSQL always uses `isTopLevel=true` for the top-level
owner (see `CommitTransaction`/`AbortTransaction` in `xact.c`).

Result string formatting — **happens after `finish_xact_command()`**:

```c
int mem_txid = bcdb_result_slot_for_txid(tx->tx_id);

// Clear the slot first to avoid stale data from a previous tx that used this slot
memset(block->result[mem_txid], 0, sizeof(block->result[mem_txid]));

// Classify the SQL command by first 6 chars (case-insensitive comparison)
int read_cmd = chk_query_type(tx->sql, "select", "SELECT");
int del_cmd  = chk_query_type(tx->sql, "delete", "DELETE");
int upd_cmd  = chk_query_type(tx->sql, "update", "UPDATE");
int ins_cmd  = chk_query_type(tx->sql, "insert", "INSERT");

int affected_rows = apply_terminal_noop ? 0 : 1;

if (read_cmd)
    strlcpy(block->result[mem_txid], tx_result,
            sizeof(block->result[mem_txid]));
else if (ins_cmd)
    snprintf(block->result[mem_txid], sizeof(block->result[mem_txid]),
             "\n\t*%s* INSERT 0 %d\n", tx->hash, affected_rows);
else if (upd_cmd)
    snprintf(block->result[mem_txid], sizeof(block->result[mem_txid]),
             "\n\t*%s* UPDATE 0 %d\n", tx->hash, affected_rows);
else if (del_cmd)
    snprintf(block->result[mem_txid], sizeof(block->result[mem_txid]),
             "\n\t*%s* DELETE 0 %d\n", tx->hash, affected_rows);
```

**`chk_query_type`** compares the first 6 bytes of `tx->sql` against both the
lowercase and uppercase form simultaneously (all of `INSERT`, `UPDATE`,
`DELETE`, `SELECT` have length 6).  Returns 1 on match, 0 otherwise.

**`bcdb_result_slot_for_txid` edge-case guard**: if `tx_id % slots` is
negative (can happen for negative tx_ids in test scenarios), the function
adds `slots` to produce a valid non-negative index:

```c
idx = tx_id % slots;
if (idx < 0) idx += slots;
```

**The `memset` before write**: required because result slots wrap around.
Slot 3 is used by tx_id=3, then by tx_id=3+slots, then 3+2*slots, etc.
Without clearing, the old result string from the previous occupant of the
slot would linger if the new result is shorter.

For SELECT, the result is `tx_result` — the 1024-byte stack buffer that was
populated after the serial gate (Step 4).  For DML, the result is the
standard command-tag format with the tx hash prefix.  If `apply_terminal_noop`
is true, `affected_rows=0` signals to middleware that the write was pre-empted.

### Step 10: Publish T3 commit markers

```c
pg_write_barrier();
__atomic_store_n(&block->result_commit_xid[mem_txid],
                 tx->xid, __ATOMIC_RELEASE);
__atomic_store_n(&block->result_committed_txid[mem_txid],
                 tx->tx_id, __ATOMIC_RELEASE);
```

`pg_write_barrier()` ensures all writes to `block->result[mem_txid]` are
visible before the commit markers are set.  Then:

1. `result_commit_xid[slot]` is stored first (XID for T3-v2 conflict skip)
2. `result_committed_txid[slot]` is stored second (the "committed" flag)

This ordering forms a **release-acquire pair** with the reader: when a reader
observes `result_committed_txid[slot] == tx_id` (ACQUIRE load), the preceding
`result_commit_xid[slot]` store (RELEASE) is guaranteed to be visible.

Any reader polling a single target slot via `bcdb_wait_until_slot_ready(tx_id)`
can now immediately read this transaction's result without waiting for any
predecessor.  The block-level submit paths intentionally do **not** use this
for whole-block completion; they wait on the global watermark instead (see
Section 5.7) so that every earlier slot is guaranteed populated.

### Step 11: Commit order serialization

```c
bcdb_wait_for_prev_committed(tx);   // wait until predecessor or later is visible
set_last_committed_txid(tx);        // advance the global watermark
```

Even though apply can now overlap (Lever D v2), the global
`last_committed_tx_id` watermark must advance in strict order.  This is because
the conflict check lower bound (`tx_id_committed`) and fresh-server guard rely
on this being a strictly monotone sequence.

`bcdb_wait_for_prev_committed()` blocks while `last_committed_tx_id < tx_id - 1`,
so it accepts either the exact predecessor or any later watermark already
advanced by recovery/error handling.  Then `set_last_committed_txid()`
atomically stores `tx_id` to
`last_committed_tx_id` and broadcasts `condCommit` if in condvar mode.

tx_id=0 skips `bcdb_wait_for_prev_committed()` via an explicit early return:
```c
if (tx->tx_id == 0) return;  /* no predecessor */
```
(`BCTxID` is `int32`, so `tx_id-1 = -1` for tx_id=0 — no unsigned underflow, but waiting for `last_committed == -1` would hang forever since -1 is the uninitialized sentinel value, so the explicit guard is essential.)

**Concrete commit-ordering walkthrough (3 workers, tx_ids 0, 1, 2):**
```
Assume apply finishes out of order: tx_2 finishes first, then tx_0, then tx_1.

tx_2 reaches bcdb_wait_for_prev_committed:
    needs last_committed >= 1,  actual last_committed = -1
    → spins/sleeps waiting

tx_0 reaches bcdb_wait_for_prev_committed:
    tx_id == 0 → early return immediately
    set_last_committed_txid(tx_0): last_committed = 0   (broadcasts condCommit)

tx_1 reaches bcdb_wait_for_prev_committed:
    needs last_committed >= 0,  actual = 0 → passes immediately
    set_last_committed_txid(tx_1): last_committed = 1   (broadcasts condCommit)

tx_2's spin unblocks:
    needs last_committed >= 1,  actual = 1 → passes
    set_last_committed_txid(tx_2): last_committed = 2

Result: watermark advances as 0 → 1 → 2 regardless of apply order.
```

### Step 12: Cleanup

```c
EndCommand(completionTag, dest);
bcdb_cleanup_optim_write_list(activeTx, true);  // drop tuple slots
delete_tx(tx);                                   // xid_map removal, then tx_pool hash removal
MemoryContextReset(bcdb_tx_context);
bcdb_ptrace_emit(tx->tx_id, num_restarts);       // write CSV row if enabled
break;  // exit the retry loop, return to tx_queue_next
```

`bcdb_cleanup_optim_write_list(tx, true)` with `drop_slots=true` calls
`ExecDropSingleTupleTableSlot()` for each buffered write's tuple slot.
`delete_tx()` performs two-phase cleanup: it first calls `remove_tx_xid_map(tx->xid)` (acquires the per-tx LWLock, removes the XID→tx mapping, then releases the lock), and only afterwards removes the `BCDBShmXact` from the `tx_pool` hash table under `tx_pool_lock`.  The ordering is required because `remove_tx_xid_map` takes the per-tx LWLock — freeing the pool entry first would allow a concurrent reader to hold a dangling reference.

---

## 7. Lever D v2 — The Serial Gate

### The problem it solves

In the original design, the serial gate waited for:

```
last_committed_tx_id + 1 == my_tx_id
```

This meant: "every tx before me must have fully committed — including apply and
`finish_xact_command`."  The consequence: apply phases were serialized.  With N
workers, the apply throughput was limited to what a single worker could sustain.
This was measured as the primary bottleneck at 8 threads.

### The Lever D v2 solution

Instead, the gate waits for:

```
published_max_tx_id + 1 >= my_tx_id
```

`published_max_tx_id` is advanced immediately after `publish_ws_tableDT`, which
happens at the **start** of the commit path (before apply).  This means:
- tx N can enter the gate as soon as tx N-1 has passed its conflict check
- tx N-1's apply can run **concurrently** with tx N's conflict check and apply

This is safe because:
1. Conflict detection only needs write-sets registered (done before gate advance)
2. Apply order within a transaction's own subtransaction is internally consistent
3. Commit order is still enforced by `bcdb_wait_for_prev_committed` at the END

**Concurrent apply timeline (2 workers):**
```
Time →  0ms     5ms     10ms    15ms    20ms    25ms    30ms

tx_10:  [gate-passes]→[pub-WS]→[adv-max]→[======== APPLY ========]→[wait-prev]→[commit]
tx_11:              →[gate: wait for max≥10]
                            ↑ tx_10 advances max; tx_11 unblocks at ~8ms
tx_11:                      [gate-passes]→[pub-WS]→[adv-max]→[==== APPLY ====]→[wait-prev]→[commit]

Old gate (last_committed):                tx_11 would wait here until tx_10's commit at ~27ms
                                          tx_11's apply could not start until ~27ms

Lever D v2:  tx_11's apply starts at ~12ms, overlapping with tx_10's apply.
             Only the final watermark step is serialized (wait-prev at ~22ms for tx_11).
```

### `bcdb_wait_for_serial_slot` internals

**Wait mode used by the DT serial gate**:

- **POLL mode** (`bcdb_serial_gate_mode=0`, the required mode): First 64 iterations
  are a tight spin with `pg_spin_delay()` (CPU-yield hint for the hyperthreading
  partner).  After 64 spins, sleeps with exponential backoff: 1us -> 2us -> 4us
  -> ... capped at 64us.  The cap prevents starvation in degenerate cases.

- **CONDVAR mode** (`bcdb_serial_gate_mode=1`): Historical GUC value only for this
  path. `set_published_max_txid()` intentionally does NOT broadcast
  `condCommit`, so the DT publish gate now always uses the poll/yield loop even
  if a stale cluster has `bcdb_serial_gate_mode=1`. The 4-node runner also forces
  and verifies `bcdb_serial_gate_mode=0` before load. This avoids the observed
  lost-wakeup hang where a worker slept on `BlockCommit` while
  `published_max == tx_id - 1`.

**Always-on watchdog**: Every 5 seconds logs `[BCDB_HANG] serial_gate_stuck`
showing tx_id, published_max, last_committed, wait duration, spin count, poll
interval.  No env-var required.

### `set_published_max_txid` atomicity

Uses a CAS-max loop that only advances `published_max` forward, never
backwards.  Why a CAS loop instead of a simple store?  Consider: tx_id=3 could
publish before tx_id=2 if tx_id=2 is in a full-restart loop.  A simple store
`published_max = 3` could race with tx_id=2 later storing `published_max = 2`,
rolling it back.  The CAS "max-advance" pattern ensures the counter
monotonically increases.

---

## 8. T3 — Per-Slot Commit Markers

### The problem T3 solves

Before T3, middleware waiting for results had to poll the global
`last_committed_tx_id` watermark.  To know that tx N's result was readable,
you had to wait until `last_committed_tx_id >= N`, which required every
transaction from 0 to N to have committed in sequence.  A slow transaction
early in a block would delay all result readback for the entire block.

**Before/after T3 — slow-tx impact:**
```
Block with 4 txs (ids 0,1,2,3). tx_1 is unusually slow (300ms vs ~5ms for others).

WITHOUT T3 (polling last_committed_tx_id):
    t=5ms:   tx_0 commits → last_committed=0
    t=5ms:   tx_2 commits → last_committed still 0 (tx_1 not done)
    t=5ms:   tx_3 commits → last_committed still 0 (tx_1 not done)
    t=300ms: tx_1 commits → last_committed=3 (jumps from 0 to 3)
    Middleware couldn't read tx_2 or tx_3 results until 300ms, despite them
    finishing at 5ms. One slow tx blocks all subsequent result readback.

WITH T3 (polling result_committed_txid[slot]):
    t=5ms:   tx_0 commits → result_committed_txid[0%slots]=0 → middleware reads tx_0
    t=5ms:   tx_2 commits → result_committed_txid[2%slots]=2 → middleware reads tx_2
    t=5ms:   tx_3 commits → result_committed_txid[3%slots]=3 → middleware reads tx_3
    t=300ms: tx_1 commits → result_committed_txid[1%slots]=1 → middleware reads tx_1
    Middleware submitted last_tx_id=3; bcdb_wait_until_slot_ready(3) fires at 300ms,
    but by then tx_0/tx_2/tx_3 slots have been ready since 5ms.
```

### How T3 works

Each BCBlock has two per-slot arrays at the very end:

```c
int32 volatile    result_committed_txid[BCDB_RESULT_RING_CAPACITY];  // initialized to -1
TransactionId volatile result_commit_xid[BCDB_RESULT_RING_CAPACITY]; // initialized to InvalidTransactionId
```

The commit sequence writes these **after** the result string:

```c
pg_write_barrier();  // ensure result is visible before markers
__atomic_store_n(&block->result_commit_xid[mem_txid],  tx->xid, __ATOMIC_RELEASE);
__atomic_store_n(&block->result_committed_txid[mem_txid], tx->tx_id, __ATOMIC_RELEASE);
```

Middleware polls `bcdb_wait_until_slot_ready(tx_id)`:

```c
// slot check:
int slot = tx_id % bcdb_get_runtime_result_ring_slots();
while (ACQUIRE_load(result_committed_txid[slot]) != tx_id)
    pg_usleep(1);
```

When the ACQUIRE load sees `tx_id`, both the result string and
`result_commit_xid` are guaranteed visible (due to the release-acquire pair).

### Write ordering: XID before committed_txid

```
Writer:  store(result_commit_xid[slot], xid, RELEASE)       <- first
         store(result_committed_txid[slot], tx_id, RELEASE)  <- second

Reader:  load(result_committed_txid[slot], ACQUIRE) -> sees tx_id
         load(result_commit_xid[slot], ACQUIRE)     -> guaranteed visible
```

A reader that observes `result_committed_txid[slot] == tx_id` is guaranteed
to see the correct XID when it loads `result_commit_xid[slot]`.

### Slot sizing

`bcdb_get_runtime_result_ring_slots()` returns `max(bcdb_result_ring_slots, 2 * num_workers)`,
clamped to `[2, BCDB_RESULT_RING_CAPACITY]`.  The minimum of `2 * num_workers` ensures
that while worker K is committing tx_id=N, worker K-1 is at most on tx_id=N-1,
so the ring never wraps around to an in-use slot.

The three ring arrays (`result`, `result_committed_txid`, `result_commit_xid`) are sized at
`BCDB_RESULT_RING_CAPACITY = 2048` rather than `MAX_TX_PER_BLOCK = 5500`.  This is safe
because the runtime slot count is always capped at `BCDB_RESULT_RING_CAPACITY`, and `2048 = 2
× bcdb_worker_count_max (1024)` covers every valid GUC combination.  Memory saving vs. the
old layout: `(5500 − 2048) × (1024 + 4 + 4) bytes ≈ 3.4 MB` per BCBlock in shared memory.
The `done_conds[]` and `txs[]` arrays are NOT ring-indexed and remain at `MAX_TX_PER_BLOCK`.

---

## 9. T3-v2 — XID-Based Conflict Skip (Infrastructure)

### The problem it addresses

Suppose tx 5 committed (PostgreSQL XID = 1000), the global watermark is only
at 4, and tx 7 is in its conflict check with `tx_id_committed = 3`.  The
write-set table has an entry from tx 5.  Under the existing check:

```c
entry->tx_id (5) > tx_id_committed (3)  =>  CONFLICT flagged
```

But tx 5's writes are already visible in tx 7's snapshot (XID 1000 <
`snap_xmin` of 1050).  The conflict flag is a false positive.

T3-v2 adds the machinery to detect this case and skip the conflict.

### Implementation in `bcdb_xid_preceded_snapshot()`

```c
static bool bcdb_xid_preceded_snapshot(BCTxID cand_id)
{
    // Read committed marker (ACQUIRE)
    published = ACQUIRE_load(blk->result_committed_txid[slot]);
    if (published != cand_id)
        return false;   // not yet committed

    // Read commit XID (guaranteed visible by release-acquire pair)
    cxid = ACQUIRE_load(blk->result_commit_xid[slot]);
    if (!TransactionIdIsValid(cxid))
        return false;

    return TransactionIdPrecedes(cxid, activeTx->snap_xmin);
}
```

`snap_xmin` is saved in Step 2 of the lifecycle (`activeTx->snap_xmin = snapshot->xmin`).

### Current wiring status: ACTIVE

`table_checkDT()` calls `bcdb_xid_preceded_snapshot` for both the `map` and
`mapB` shard conflict checks.  When a candidate conflict entry is found, the
check is:

```c
if (found && (entry->tx_id < activeTx->tx_id) &&
             (entry->tx_id > activeTx->tx_id_committed))
{
    cand_id = entry->tx_id;
    SpinLockRelease(partition_lock);
    /* T3-v2: skip if candidate committed before our snapshot xmin. */
    if (bcdb_xid_preceded_snapshot(cand_id))
        goto check_done;
    return true;  /* genuine conflict */
}
```

The same pattern is applied for the `mapB` early-exit path.  `check_done` is a
label that falls through to `return false` so no spurious conflict is raised.

---

## 10. `advance_last_committed_txid` (Infrastructure)

### The problem it addresses

`bcdb_wait_for_prev_committed()` + `set_last_committed_txid()` is an O(N)
serial bottleneck.  Every tx must wait for its predecessor before advancing the
watermark.  `advance_last_committed_txid()` replaces this with a lock-free CAS
prefix scan where the fastest-finishing tx amortises the advance for all of its
already-committed successors.

### Implementation

```c
void advance_last_committed_txid(BCDBShmXact *tx)
{
    BCTxID my_id = tx->tx_id;
    BCTxID prev  = my_id - 1;

    // Try to advance from (my_id - 1) to my_id atomically
    if (!__sync_bool_compare_and_swap(&blk->last_committed_tx_id, prev, my_id))
        return;  // predecessor not yet committed; it will carry the scan

    // CAS succeeded. Now eagerly scan forward:
    cur = my_id;
    for (int step = 0; step < slots; step++) {
        BCTxID next_id = cur + 1;
        // Check if next_id is already committed via T3 per-slot marker
        published = ACQUIRE_load(blk->result_committed_txid[next_id % slots]);
        if (published != next_id) break;
        // Try to advance to next_id
        if (!__sync_bool_compare_and_swap(&blk->last_committed_tx_id, cur, next_id))
            break;  // another worker beat us
        cur = next_id;
    }
}
```

The CAS on the initial step ensures only one thread "owns" the advance from
`prev` to `my_id`.  If the CAS fails (predecessor not yet committed), the
function returns immediately — the predecessor will eventually run the same
function and the scan will include this slot.

**Concrete scan example (4 workers, txs 10–13):**
```
Initial state: last_committed = 9.

tx_11 finishes apply first:
    CAS(last_committed, 10, 11) → FAILS (current=9, expected 10) → return immediately.

tx_13 finishes apply:
    CAS(last_committed, 12, 13) → FAILS (current=9, expected 12) → return immediately.

tx_10 finishes apply:
    CAS(last_committed, 9, 10) → SUCCEEDS → scan forward:
        check result_committed_txid[11 % slots] → equals 11 → YES
        CAS(last_committed, 10, 11) → SUCCEEDS
        check result_committed_txid[12 % slots] → equals -1 (not done) → STOP
    last_committed = 11

tx_12 finishes apply:
    CAS(last_committed, 11, 12) → SUCCEEDS → scan forward:
        check result_committed_txid[13 % slots] → equals 13 → YES
        CAS(last_committed, 12, 13) → SUCCEEDS
        check result_committed_txid[14 % slots] → not 14 → STOP
    last_committed = 13

Net effect: tx_10's finish call did the work of advancing through tx_11;
tx_12's call did the work for tx_13. No O(N) sequential waiting.
```

### Current wiring status: NOT YET CALLED

Declared in `shm_block.h`, implemented in `shm_block.c`, with the comment:

> T3: non-blocking replacement for the old
> bcdb_wait_for_prev_committed + set_last_committed_txid pair.

`worker.c` still calls `bcdb_wait_for_prev_committed(tx); set_last_committed_txid(tx);`.

---

## 11. Apply Retry Loop

### Why retries are needed

Multiple workers applying concurrently (Lever D v2) can encounter:

- **Unique constraint violations**: two txs inserting the same key, where the
  later one arrives at heap insert while the earlier one's index entry has just
  landed but isn't yet committed.
- **Lock waits / TM_BEING_MODIFIED**: a tuple being modified by another tx's
  apply that has not yet committed.

Rather than restarting the entire transaction (re-running parse/plan/portal-run),
the retry loop re-runs only the apply step within a subtransaction.

### Implementation

```c
#define BCDB_APPLY_RETRY_MAX 64
#define BCDB_APPLY_RETRY_BACKOFF_MAX_US 256

for (;;) {
    BeginInternalSubTransaction("bcdb_apply_retry");
    PG_TRY() {
        apply_ok = apply_optim_writes();
        if (apply_ok)
            ReleaseCurrentSubTransaction();
        else
            RollbackAndReleaseCurrentSubTransaction();
    }
    PG_CATCH() { RollbackAndReleaseCurrentSubTransaction(); PG_RE_THROW(); }
    PG_END_TRY();

    if (apply_ok) return true;

    retries++;
    if (retries > BCDB_APPLY_RETRY_MAX) {
        if (apply_failed_unique) *nonretryable_error = true;
        return false;
    }

    pg_usleep(backoff_us);  // 1->2->4->...->256 us
    backoff_us = min(backoff_us * 2, BCDB_APPLY_RETRY_BACKOFF_MAX_US);
}
```

Each attempt runs inside `BeginInternalSubTransaction / Release` (commit) or
`RollbackAndRelease` (failure).  This scopes the partial heap writes so they
are fully rolled back if apply fails.

### `apply_terminal_noop`

After exhausting 64 retries with a **unique violation**: `apply_nonretryable = true`
-> `apply_terminal_noop = true`.  The transaction "commits" with
`affected_rows=0`.  This is correct: a unique violation in deterministic
execution means this insert was pre-empted by a committed predecessor — the
write is effectively a no-op and the transaction should not error.

Any other error after 64 retries: `rw_conflicts = 1; continue;` — triggers
a full transaction restart.

**Terminal noop end-to-end example:**
```
tx_id=5:  INSERT INTO t VALUES(42)  (key=42)
tx_id=6:  INSERT INTO t VALUES(42)  (same key=42 — blockchain clients may send this deliberately)

tx_id=5 apply: table_insert succeeds → key=42 committed in index.

tx_id=6 apply attempt 1: btree unique check finds key=42 already present
    → UniqueViolation exception → RollbackAndReleaseCurrentSubTransaction()
    apply_failed_unique = true

tx_id=6 apply attempts 2..64: key=42 is permanently committed (tx_5 won't roll back)
    → same UniqueViolation every time

After 64 retries:
    apply_nonretryable = true  →  apply_terminal_noop = true
    affected_rows = 0
    result[slot] = "\n\t*00000006* INSERT 0 0\n"

tx_id=6 "commits" normally — watermark advances, T3 slot is set.
Middleware receives: affected_rows=0 → treats as accepted but no-op.
No error is raised; determinism is preserved.
```

---

## 12. Phase Tracer

### Activation

```bash
BCDB_PHASE_TRACE=/tmp/trace_prefix
```

Each worker backend opens `<prefix>.<pid>` for line-buffered (`_IOLBF`)
CSV writes.  Zero overhead when disabled (a `cached int` env-var check evaluated
once per worker startup).

### 7 main phases (columns 3–9 in CSV)

| CSV column | Phase | What it covers |
|---|---|---|
| `parse_plan_us` | `BCDB_PHASE_PARSE_PLAN` | `pg_parse_query` → analyze → plan → `CreatePortal` → `PortalStart`; also covers post-run executor cleanup and `bcdb_maybe_enqueue_deferred_delete0_by_key` |
| `portal_run_us` | `BCDB_PHASE_PORTAL_RUN` | `PortalRun()` — the actual SQL execution including DML buffering |
| `gate_us` | `BCDB_PHASE_GATE` | Waiting in `bcdb_wait_for_serial_slot` |
| `conflict_us` | `BCDB_PHASE_CONFLICT` | `conflict_checkDT()` |
| `apply_us` | `BCDB_PHASE_APPLY` | `bcdb_apply_optim_writes_with_retry()` including all subtransaction retries |
| `finish_us` | `BCDB_PHASE_FINISH` | Portal resource release → `finish_xact_command` → result write → T3 publish → prev_commit wait → `set_last_committed_txid` → `EndCommand` → `delete_tx` → `MemoryContextReset` |
| `total_us` | `BCDB_PHASE_TOTAL` | End-to-end wall time from `bcdb_worker_process_tx_dt` entry to `break` (includes queue pop wait for the *next* tx, if pending) |

### 17 fine-grained sub-phase metrics (columns 10–26)

| CSV column | Metric | What it measures |
|---|---|---|
| `queue_pop_wait_us` | `METRIC_QUEUE_POP_WAIT_US` | Time the worker spent blocked in `tx_queue_next` between the previous and current tx |
| `publish_ws_us` | `METRIC_PUBLISH_WS_US` | Time inside `publish_ws_tableDT` (hash-table insert under partition locks) |
| `publish_hash_clear_us` | `METRIC_PUBLISH_HASH_CLEAR_US` | Time clearing the newly-active shard at an epoch boundary in `publish_ws_tableDT` |
| `prev_apply_wait_us` | `METRIC_PRE_APPLY_WAIT_US` | Reserved for any pre-apply wait (currently 0 — the v2-fix1 wait was removed) |
| `conflict_ws_us` | `METRIC_CONFLICT_WS_US` | Time scanning ws_table_record and probing ws_table map/mapB |
| `conflict_rs_us` | `METRIC_CONFLICT_RS_US` | Time scanning rs_table_record and probing ws_table |
| `apply_insert_us` | `METRIC_APPLY_INSERT_US` | Time in `apply_optim_insert` across all attempts |
| `apply_update_us` | `METRIC_APPLY_UPDATE_US` | Time in `apply_optim_update` across all attempts |
| `apply_delete_us` | `METRIC_APPLY_DELETE_US` | Time in `apply_optim_delete` / `apply_deferred_delete_by_key` |
| `apply_update_wait_us` | `METRIC_APPLY_UPDATE_WAIT_US` | Accumulated wait inside `apply_optim_update` for `TM_BEING_MODIFIED` retries |
| `apply_delete_wait_us` | `METRIC_APPLY_DELETE_WAIT_US` | Same for `apply_optim_delete` |
| `apply_delete_lookup_us` | `METRIC_APPLY_DELETE_LOOKUP_US` | Time for heap/index lookup during deferred delete-by-key |
| `apply_merkle_prep_us` | `METRIC_APPLY_MERKLE_PREP_US` | Time calling `merkle_record_update` per DML |
| `apply_merkle_update_us` | `METRIC_APPLY_MERKLE_UPDATE_US` | Time in `apply_merkle_changeset` (batch XOR into Merkle index) |
| `finish_result_us` | `METRIC_FINISH_RESULT_US` | Time to memset + format + write `block->result[slot]` + store T3 markers |
| `finish_prev_commit_wait_us` | `METRIC_FINISH_PREV_COMMIT_WAIT_US` | Time spent in `bcdb_wait_for_prev_committed` |
| `finish_publish_us` | `METRIC_FINISH_PUBLISH_US` | Time in `bcdb_wait_for_prev_committed` + `set_last_committed_txid` combined |

**Total: 17 fine-grained metrics** (`BCDB_PTRACE_METRIC_COUNT = 17`).

### 13 event counters (columns 27–39)

| CSV column | Counter | What it counts |
|---|---|---|
| `ws_publish_entries` | `COUNTER_WS_PUBLISH_ENTRIES` | Number of write-set entries published in `publish_ws_tableDT` |
| `ws_conflict_checks` | `COUNTER_WS_CONFLICT_CHECKS` | Number of ws_table_record entries probed in `conflict_checkDT` |
| `rs_conflict_checks` | `COUNTER_RS_CONFLICT_CHECKS` | Number of rs_table_record entries probed |
| `apply_insert_count` | `COUNTER_APPLY_INSERT_COUNT` | Successful insert operations in apply |
| `apply_update_count` | `COUNTER_APPLY_UPDATE_COUNT` | Successful update operations |
| `apply_delete_count` | `COUNTER_APPLY_DELETE_COUNT` | Successful delete operations |
| `apply_update_tm_being_modified_count` | `COUNTER_APPLY_UPDATE_TM_BEING_MODIFIED_COUNT` | `TM_BEING_MODIFIED` encounters during update apply |
| `apply_delete_tm_being_modified_count` | `COUNTER_APPLY_DELETE_TM_BEING_MODIFIED_COUNT` | Same for delete |
| `apply_update_wait_incident_count` | `COUNTER_APPLY_UPDATE_WAIT_INCIDENT_COUNT` | Number of times update had to wait (spin + retry) |
| `apply_delete_wait_incident_count` | `COUNTER_APPLY_DELETE_WAIT_INCIDENT_COUNT` | Same for delete |
| `merkle_update_count` | `COUNTER_MERKLE_UPDATE_COUNT` | Number of `PendingMerkleUpdate` records applied |
| `apply_retry_count` | `COUNTER_APPLY_RETRY_COUNT` | Total subtransaction retries across the full tx (includes apply retries AND full tx restarts) |
| `publish_hash_clear_count` | `COUNTER_PUBLISH_HASH_CLEAR_COUNT` | Number of epoch ping-pong map clears triggered |

**Sample CSV row** (from `BCDB_PHASE_TRACE=/tmp/trace`):
```
# Header (written once per worker file):
tx_id,restarts,parse_plan_us,portal_run_us,gate_us,conflict_us,apply_us,finish_us,total_us,\
queue_pop_wait_us,publish_ws_us,publish_hash_clear_us,prev_apply_wait_us,conflict_ws_us,\
conflict_rs_us,apply_insert_us,apply_update_us,apply_delete_us,apply_update_wait_us,\
apply_delete_wait_us,apply_delete_lookup_us,apply_merkle_prep_us,apply_merkle_update_us,\
finish_result_us,finish_prev_commit_wait_us,finish_publish_us,ws_publish_entries,\
ws_conflict_checks,rs_conflict_checks,apply_insert_count,...

# Example data row (UPDATE tx, 1-thread run):
42,0,850,1200,0,12,380,920,3400, 2100,18,0,0,12,0, 0,380,0,0,0,0,5,12, 40,60,62, 2,2,0,0,1,0,...

# Reading the row:
#   tx_id=42, 0 restarts
#   parse_plan=850µs, portal_run=1200µs (SQL exec dominated at 1 thread)
#   gate=0µs (gate passed immediately — no contention at 1 thread)
#   conflict=12µs, apply=380µs (1 UPDATE)
#   finish=920µs (prev_commit_wait=60µs was the main component)
#   queue_pop_wait=2100µs (worker spent 2.1ms idle waiting for next tx)
```

### Key measurement findings (2026-04-18)

Phase trace analysis on real benchmark runs revealed:

- **1 thread**: `portal_run_us` = 68% of total — SQL execution dominates
- **8 threads**: `gate_us` = 83% of total — gate is the bottleneck
- **Merkle**: only 6% of total — batched-Merkle optimization was de-prioritized
- After Lever D v2: apply overlap freed gate time, `finish_us` improved with T3

---

## 13. Watchdogs and Diagnostics

### Always-on 5-second hang watchdogs

Three places fire unconditionally every 5s when stuck:

**1. `bcdb_wait_for_serial_slot`**:
```
[BCDB_HANG] serial_gate_stuck pid=X txid=N published_max=M last_committed=M
            waited_us=5000000 spins=64 poll_us=64
```
This tells you: which tx is stuck, what the gate counter is, and whether the
predecessor is stuck itself vs. still in apply.

**2. `bcdb_wait_for_prev_committed`**:
```
[BCDB_HANG] prev_commit_stuck pid=X txid=N need_committed=N-1
            last_committed=M published_max=P waited_us=5000000
```
Shows exactly which predecessor is missing.  The `published_max` field
distinguishes "predecessor not yet in apply" (published_max < N-1) from
"predecessor stuck in its own commit serialization" (published_max >= N-1
but last_committed stalled).

**3. Middleware slot wait**: similar pattern for T3 per-slot polling.

**Reading a hang message — diagnostic walkthrough:**
```
LOG: [BCDB_HANG] prev_commit_stuck pid=17432 txid=8 need_committed=7
                 last_committed=5 published_max=7 waited_us=5000000

Interpretation:
  published_max=7:  tx_7 has passed conflict check, published write-set, and
                    released the gate for tx_8. tx_7 is IN apply or finish.
  last_committed=5: the global watermark is stuck at 5. tx_6 committed; tx_7 has not.
  need_committed=7: tx_8 is waiting for tx_7's commit (last_committed to reach 7).

Root cause narrowed down: tx_7 is stuck somewhere between its gate release and
its set_last_committed_txid call (i.e., stuck in apply or bcdb_wait_for_prev_committed).

Next: check if tx_7 has its own [BCDB_HANG] serial_gate_stuck log. If no:
    tx_7 passed its gate but is stuck in apply → look for lock waits or
    TM_BEING_MODIFIED loops in tx_7's worker process (pid can be found via
    BCDBShmXact.worker_pid if available, or by matching the wait pattern).
    
LOG: [BCDB_HANG] serial_gate_stuck pid=17429 txid=5 published_max=3
                 last_committed=3 waited_us=5000000 spins=64 poll_us=64

Interpretation:
  txid=5 is waiting for published_max ≥ 4.
  published_max=3: tx_4 has NOT yet published its write-set.
  last_committed=3: consistent — tx_4 is stuck even before reaching publish.
  → tx_4 is stuck in its own serial gate or in the optimistic run (parse/plan/portal).
```

**Why always-on?**  In production, by the time someone suspects a hang, they
may not have any debug env-vars set.  The 5-second watchdog is visible in
standard PostgreSQL log output without any setup.

### `BCDB_GATE_DEBUG=1`

Per-second log of gate wait state, plus a final `[BCDB_GATE] serial_gate_done`
log when a wait exceeds 100ms.  More granular than the watchdog; use during
active debugging.

### `BCDB_FLOW_DEBUG=1`

Logs at every major phase transition per transaction:
`get_write_set_begin`, `portal_run_enter/exit`, `apply_attempt_enter/exit`,
`apply_retry_exhausted`, `apply_nonretryable_finalize_noop`, `tx_error`.

### Fresh-server guard

Detects the common misconfiguration where a client submits tx_ids starting at
N > 0 on a fresh server (e.g., `DET_START_SEQ` was set after restart):

1. If `published_max < 0` AND `last_committed < 0` AND `tx_id > 0`: start a
   2-second grace timer.
2. Every 1ms, look up hash `"00000000"` (the key for tx_id=0) in `tx_pool`.
3. If tx_id=0 is found: normal parallel startup (client submitted txs 0..N
   concurrently and tx 0 is just slow).  Clear the guard.
4. If 2 seconds pass with no tx_id=0 and no watermark movement: raise a clear
   error with a hint about `DET_START_SEQ`.

---

## 14. CV Layout Hazard — Wave 2.9 PANIC Class

### What happened

During refactoring, new fields were inserted into `BCBlock` *before* the
`ConditionVariable` fields.  This caused a PANIC at server startup.

### Why it PANICs

PostgreSQL's `ConditionVariable` embeds an `slock_t` (spinlock mutex) at a
specific byte offset within the struct.  `ConditionVariableInit()` initializes
the mutex at the actual runtime address of the struct in shared memory.

**The problem**: if you insert a new field *before* a `ConditionVariable` in
the struct definition, the offset of that ConditionVariable from the struct base
changes.  Since shared memory is mapped at the same physical pages by all
processes, the mutex byte for a CV ends up at the wrong offset if any process
uses a different struct layout.  This manifests as spinlock corruption.

In practice, even within a single server with consistent binaries, PostgreSQL
may partially initialize the shared-memory struct with the new layout while
some cached state still references the old offsets, causing corruption on the
very first `ConditionVariablePrepareToSleep` call.

### The fix and the rule

New fields added to `BCBlock` **must be placed AFTER `result[]`**.  The
`result[]` array is the largest field in the struct.  Adding after it cannot
change the offset of any earlier field.  The comment in `shm_block.h`:

```c
/*
 * Placed AFTER result[] on purpose — result[] is the largest array in
 * the struct, so adding a field after it cannot shift the offset of
 * any earlier ConditionVariable's internal mutex byte. This is the
 * same CV-layout hazard that caused the shm_block.h wave 2.9 PANIC.
 */
int volatile published_max_tx_id;
```

**Concrete struct layout — broken vs safe:**
```c
// BROKEN: new field inserted before condCommit
typedef struct {
    BCBlockID  id;                     // offset 0
    int        num_tx;                 // offset 8
    int        new_counter;            // ← NEW (breaks everything after this)
    int        last_committed_tx_id;   // was offset 16, now offset 24
    ConditionVariable condCommit;      // ← mutex byte now at wrong address!
    ...
} BCBlock;
// ConditionVariableInit() initialized condCommit's mutex at the old address.
// Now any ConditionVariablePrepareToSleep acquires a different memory location
// → spinlock corruption → PANIC on first postmaster startup.

// SAFE: new field placed after result[]
typedef struct {
    BCBlockID  id;
    int        num_tx;
    int        last_committed_tx_id;
    ConditionVariable condCommit;      // ← offset unchanged ✓
    char       result[5500][1024];     // ← largest array, "anchor" for new fields
    int        new_counter;            // ← NEW: after result[], safe ✓
    int        published_max_tx_id;    // also after result[], safe ✓
    int32      result_committed_txid[5500]; // safe ✓
} BCBlock;
```

This is why `published_max_tx_id`, `result_committed_txid[]`, and
`result_commit_xid[]` are all at the very end of the struct.  Any future field
additions to BCBlock must follow the same rule.

---

## 15. Configuration Reference

### GUCs

For the BCDB-specific knobs in this checkout, the important GUC classes are:

- **PGC_POSTMASTER**: cluster-wide, set before postmaster startup, read-only thereafter
- **PGC_BACKEND**: startup-time per-connection values, commonly pushed into worker sessions by BCDB control code
- **PGC_USERSET**: normal session GUCs that clients can change with `SET` / `set_config(...)`

#### PGC_POSTMASTER GUCs (cluster-wide)

Registered in `guc.c` under `DEVELOPER_OPTIONS`. Cannot be changed after postmaster startup.

| GUC | Type | Default | Range | Meaning |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `bcdb_worker_count` | int | `2` | 1–1024 | Number of worker backends; seeds `blksize` at block init via `get_block_by_id(1, true)` → `blksize = bcdb_worker_count` |
| `bcdb_dt_conflict_tracking` | bool | `off` | — | Enables write-set reservation and DT conflict detection; off = serial ordering only, no WS publication, no hash tables allocated |
| `bcdb_serial_gate_mode` | int | `0` | 0–1 | 0=`BCDB_SERIAL_GATE_MODE_POLL`; 1 is accepted for compatibility but `bcdb_wait_for_serial_slot` still uses the poll/yield loop because `set_published_max_txid()` does not broadcast `condCommit` |
| `bcdb_dt_hashtab_switch_threshold` | int | `1500` | 1–INT_MAX | Divisor for computing the ping-pong epoch number from a tx_id (`epoch = tx_id / threshold`); even epochs use `map`, odd epochs use `mapB`; at each epoch boundary the newly-active shard is cleared |
| `bcdb_result_ring_slots` | int | `128` | 2–2048 | **Target** result ring size; actual runtime size = `max(this, 2 * bcdb_worker_count)` enforced by `bcdb_get_runtime_result_ring_slots()`; modulo wrapping gives `mem_txid = tx_id % ring_slots` |

#### PGC_BACKEND GUCs (per-connection, GUC_REPORT)

Written by the master into each worker connection's GUC state at connection setup.
`GUC_REPORT` causes PostgreSQL to send the value back to the client in the parameter
status message; the master uses this to confirm the worker received the correct values.

| GUC | Type | Default | Meaning |
|---|---|---|---|
| `is_bcdb_master` | bool | `false` | Set to true on the master connection; gates master-only code paths in `middleware.c` |
| `is_bcdb_worker` | bool | `false` | Set to true on each worker connection; gates `bcdb_worker_process_tx_dt` dispatch in `worker.c`; saved/restored by `safedb_txdt` around direct-execution calls |
| `OEP_mode` | bool | `false` | Optimistic Execution Pipeline mode flag; controls whether the worker uses the `optim_write_list` path or immediate apply |
| `blocksize` | int | `0` | The current block's transaction capacity (`blksize`); sent to worker so it knows how many transactions are in the block |
| `worker_id` | int | `0` | Zero-based worker index within the block (0 to `bcdb_worker_count - 1`); used to stripe work across workers |

#### PGC_USERSET GUCs (per-session, client-settable)

| GUC | Type | Default | Meaning |
|---|---|---|---|
| `bcdb_client_public_key` | string | `''` | Base64 DER RSA public key used by `postgres.c` when verifying `##`-delimited signatures; empty means use the built-in fallback development key |
| `bcdb_enforce_signatures` | bool | `false` | Controls whether `postgres.c` rejects a signed query whose verification fails (`true`) or only warns and continues (`false`) |

**Example handoff sequence** (master sets up a new worker connection):
```
SET is_bcdb_worker = true;      -- GUC_REPORT → master sees confirmation
SET OEP_mode = true;
SET blocksize = 64;
SET worker_id = 3;
-- Now send transaction SQL
```

### Environment variables (cached at first access per process)

All env vars are evaluated **once** per process via a `static int cached = -1` pattern —
the first call to the corresponding `_enabled()` function reads `getenv()` and stores 0 or 1.
Changing an env var after a process starts has **no effect** until the process restarts.

| Variable | Default | Truthy condition | Meaning |
|---|---|---|---|
| `BCDB_PHASE_TRACE=<path-prefix>` | unset | any non-empty value | Enables per-tx phase tracer; each worker backend appends CSV to `<prefix>.<pid>`; see Section 12 for full column list |
| `BCDB_BLOCK_PROFILE=1` | 0 (off) | non-empty, not `0/n/N/f/F` | Emits one `PROFILE_BCDB_BLOCK` log line per `bcdb_block_submit_results()` call, splitting JSON parse, block parse, enqueue, slot wait, result formatting, and total time |
| `BCDB_POLL_MAX_US=<1..64>` | `64` | numeric | Caps adaptive poll sleeps used by deterministic ordered waits (`bcdb_wait_until_slot_ready`, `bcdb_wait_until_committed`, serial-gate waits). Lower values are experimental and can reduce throughput by increasing CPU pressure |
| `BCDB_GATE_DEBUG=1` | 0 (off) | non-empty, not `0/n/N/f/F` | Per-second gate diagnostic `LOG` lines + "done" log for waits exceeding ~100ms; opt-in supplement to the always-on 5s hang watchdog |
| `BCDB_FLOW_DEBUG=1` | 0 (off) | non-empty, not `0/n/N/f/F` | Per-phase-transition `LOG` messages in both `worker.c` and `shm_transaction.c`; produces one log line per major state transition per transaction |
| `BCDB_TRACE_TIMESTAMPS=1` | 0 (off) | non-empty, not `0/n/N/f/F` | Legacy hot-path latency trace; appends human-readable timing to `/tmp/timestamps.txt`; one `open/fprintf/close` per transaction (expensive under load) |
| `BCDB_XIDMAP_DEBUG=1` | 0 (off) | non-empty, not `"0"` | Logs XID-map probe and hit events via `BCDB_XIDMAP_LOG`; used to debug T3-v2 XID-based conflict skip (`bcdb_xid_preceded_snapshot`) |
| `BCDB_STEP1_TM_PROBE=0` | 1 (**on**) | truthy if var unset or empty | **Default-on** guard: enables Step 1 tuple-modified probing during apply; set to `0/n/N/f/F` to disable; note the inverted default vs other vars |

**Caching subtlety — `BCDB_XIDMAP_DEBUG` vs others:**
- Most env vars: `*v != '0' && *v != 'n' ...` — rejects any single-char falsy prefix
- `BCDB_XIDMAP_DEBUG`: uses `strcmp(v, "0") != 0` — only the exact string `"0"` is falsy; `"00"` or `"false"` would be truthy
- `BCDB_STEP1_TM_PROBE`: inverted logic — `cached = 1` when `v == NULL || v[0] == '\0'`; explicitly disabled by setting to `0/n/N/f/F`

**2026-04-29 distributed profiling note.** On the strict 4-node
Raft+BCDB+Kafka YCSB-A run at pool/batch/window `256/256/4096`,
`PROFILE_BCDB_BLOCK` showed that full-run JSON parsing was only about
16-18 ms per node and block-object construction about 37-44 ms per node.
The dominant wall time was `wait_slot_ms` (~3.1-3.6 s), meaning
`bcdb_block_submit_results()` was mostly waiting for deterministic worker
results to become ready. `BCDB_PHASE_TRACE` then showed that the pressure is
inside deterministic ordered handoff/gate behavior, while raw apply and Merkle
update time are comparatively small. An 8-us `BCDB_POLL_MAX_US` probe preserved
Merkle consistency but reduced throughput from the ~5.2k class to ~4.7k TPS, so
the default remains 64 us.

---

## 16. Detailed Module Reference

### shm_block.c / shm_block.h

#### `get_block1_cached(bool create)` — static inline

Avoids a `block_pool_lock` + hash lookup on every access to the sentinel block.
The cache (`block1_cache`) is a process-local static pointer initialized to
NULL.  On first call, invokes `get_block_by_id(1, create)` and caches the
result.  Subsequent calls return the cached pointer immediately.

Why this matters: `get_last_committed_txid()`, `get_published_max_txid()`,
`set_last_committed_txid()`, `get_blksz()`, and all sentinel accessors are
called on every transaction's hot path — potentially thousands of times per
second per worker.  Without caching, each would acquire `block_pool_lock` and
do a hash lookup.

#### `create_block_pool()`

Called once from `ipci.c` at postmaster startup.  Initializes:
- `BlockMeta` with `global_bmin=1`, `global_bmax=0`, zeroed counters
- All `NUM_BMIN_COND` condition variables in `block_meta->conds[]`
- `block_pool_lock` spinlock
- `block_pool` fixed-size shared-memory hash table (capacity `MAX_NUM_BLOCKS`)

**Critical note**: `set_blksz(1)` is intentionally NOT called here.  At this
point the hash table is allocated but empty — block 1 does not yet exist.
Calling `set_blksz(1)` would call `get_block1_cached(true)` ->
`get_block_by_id(1, true)` which tries to HASH_ENTER into the table before it
is ready, causing a SIGSEGV.  Block 1 is created lazily on first access.

#### `get_block_by_id(BCBlockID id, bool create_if_not_found)`

Primary shared-memory block accessor.  Acquires `block_pool_lock` for the
hash search to prevent concurrent create/find races.

For newly created blocks (found=false), initializes:
- `id`, `num_tx=0`, `num_ready=0`, `num_finished=0`
- `last_committed_tx_id = -1` (no tx committed yet)
- `published_max_tx_id = -1` (Lever D v2; no tx published yet)
- All 3 ConditionVariables initialized
- `blksize = (id == 1) ? bcdb_worker_count : 0` (only sentinel gets real blksize)
- All `done_conds[i]`, `result[i]` cleared
- All `result_committed_txid[i] = -1`, `result_commit_xid[i] = InvalidTransactionId`

Also calls `bcdb_worker_init()` unconditionally.

#### `set_last_committed_txid(BCDBShmXact *tx)`

Advances the global commit watermark with `__atomic_store_n` RELEASE ordering.
Also updates `block_meta->num_committed`.  In condvar mode, broadcasts
`condCommit`.  The old `block_pool_lock`-based variant has been removed; atomic
operations are sufficient for a simple monotone counter.

#### `advance_last_committed_txid(BCDBShmXact *tx)` — NOT YET CALLED

The lock-free CAS prefix scan.  Full design in Section 10.  Declared in
`shm_block.h`; implemented; not yet wired into `worker.c`.

#### `set_published_max_txid(BCDBShmXact *tx)` / `get_published_max_txid(BCDBShmXact *tx)`

Lever D v2 gate accessors.  `set_published_max_txid` uses a CAS-max loop (only
advances, never retreats).  `get_published_max_txid` uses an ACQUIRE load.
Both work on the sentinel block 1.  The `(void) tx` in the getter discards the
unused parameter — the function always reads from block 1.

#### `bcdb_get_result_ring_slots()` / `bcdb_get_runtime_result_ring_slots()`

`bcdb_get_result_ring_slots()` clamps `bcdb_result_ring_slots` to
`[2, BCDB_RESULT_RING_CAPACITY]`.

`bcdb_get_runtime_result_ring_slots()` additionally enforces a floor of
`2 * num_workers` to guarantee the ring doesn't wrap before results are read.
Both `printtup.c` and `worker.c` use the runtime variant for consistent slot
assignment.

#### `set_blksz()` / `get_blksz()`

Accessors for the sentinel block's `blksize` field.  These are called from
single-threaded startup paths and the occasional config reload — no lock needed
in current usage.  `get_blksz()` falls through to `get_block1_cached(true)` if
the cached block doesn't exist yet (handles the early-init window).

---

### worker.c

#### `bcdb_worker_init()`

Idempotent — returns true immediately if contexts already exist.  Creates two
PostgreSQL `AllocSet` memory contexts:
- `bcdb_worker_context` under `TopMemoryContext` — long-lived, for worker-global allocations
- `bcdb_tx_context` under `TopMemoryContext` — per-transaction, reset via
  `MemoryContextReset(bcdb_tx_context)` at end of each transaction

Seeds the random number generator with `getpid()`.

#### Phase tracer internals

Process-local static state:
- `bcdb_ptrace_fp` — file pointer (NULL when disabled)
- `bcdb_ptrace_phase_start[BCDB_PHASE_COUNT]` — per-phase `struct timespec` start timestamps (7 entries: PARSE_PLAN, PORTAL_RUN, GATE, CONFLICT, APPLY, FINISH, TOTAL)
- `bcdb_ptrace_phase_us[BCDB_PHASE_COUNT]` — per-phase accumulated microseconds (7 entries)
- `bcdb_ptrace_metric_us[BCDB_PTRACE_METRIC_COUNT]` — fine-grained metric accumulator (17 entries; see Section 12)
- `bcdb_ptrace_counter[BCDB_PTRACE_COUNTER_COUNT]` — event counter accumulator (13 entries; see Section 12)
- `bcdb_ptrace_pending_queue_pop_wait_us` — cross-transaction queue wait

`bcdb_ptrace_reset()` zeroes all accumulators at the start of each transaction.
It copies `pending_queue_pop_wait_us` into `metric_us[QUEUE_POP_WAIT]` first —
the queue wait time accumulated *between* transactions must be captured for the
*next* transaction's row.

`PTRACE_BEGIN(phase)` / `PTRACE_END(phase)` macros call `clock_gettime` and
accumulate delta_us.  Zero overhead when disabled.

`bcdb_ptrace_emit(tx_id, restarts)` opens the file lazily, writes the header
once, then writes one CSV row per transaction.  Line-buffered (`_IOLBF`) for
per-row flushes without explicit `fflush`.

#### `bcdb_apply_optim_writes_with_retry()`

Full design in Section 11.  Implementation detail: after
`RollbackAndReleaseCurrentSubTransaction()`, the code explicitly restores
`CurrentMemoryContext` and `CurrentResourceOwner`.  PostgreSQL's subtransaction
machinery may switch these during the subtransaction lifecycle; restoring them
is required for the outer transaction to continue correctly.

#### `bcdb_wait_for_serial_slot()`

Full design in Section 7.  The always-on 5s watchdog is initialized as
`next_warn_us = wait_start_us + 5000000` regardless of `BCDB_GATE_DEBUG`.
The per-second opt-in `next_log_us` is only set when `gate_debug=true`.

The fresh-server guard (Section 13) is checked every iteration when active.

#### `bcdb_wait_for_prev_committed()`

Full design in Section 6 Step 11.  Uses 128 spins before switching to sleep
(vs 64 in the gate) because the predecessor is typically farther ahead and the
wait is expected to be longer.  Backoff caps at 64us.

The always-on watchdog prints `published_max` in addition to `last_committed`,
which is critical for diagnosing whether the predecessor is:
- Stuck in its serial gate (published_max hasn't advanced past it)
- Stuck in its apply or finish_xact_command (published_max advanced but last_committed hasn't)

#### `get_write_set(BCDBShmXact *tx, Snapshot snapshot)`

Runs one SQL statement through the full parse -> analyze -> plan -> portal ->
run pipeline with a fixed snapshot.  Key behaviors:
- `assert(parsetree_list->length == 1)` — only single-statement txs in DT path
- `PortalStart(portal, NULL, 0, snapshot)` — pins the portal to caller's snapshot
- Returns after `PortalRun` completes, **without** calling `ExecutorFinish/End` or
  `FreeQueryDesc`

**Important**: `ExecutorFinish`, `ExecutorEnd`, and `FreeQueryDesc` are called by the
**caller** (`bcdb_worker_process_tx_dt`) after `get_write_set` returns, using a
save/restore pattern around the resource owner:
```c
/* in bcdb_worker_process_tx_dt, after get_write_set returns: */
ExecutorFinish(portal->queryDesc);
ExecutorEnd(portal->queryDesc);
FreeQueryDesc(portal->queryDesc);
portal->queryDesc = NULL;
```
This ensures the executor resources are released in the correct resource owner context
even when `get_write_set` is called from a subtransaction or retry loop.

#### `bcdb_maybe_enqueue_deferred_delete0_by_key()`

Handles YCSB DELETE-0 edge case: a DELETE that returns 0 rows because the key
doesn't exist yet.  If an earlier-ordered tx is about to insert that key, this
DELETE should conflict with it.  The function:
1. Confirms the SQL starts with `DELETE` (case-insensitive) and `completionTag` is "DELETE 0"
2. Calls `parse_ycsb_key_eq_int32(sql, &keyval)` to extract the integer key value from `YCSB_KEY = N`; returns early if parse fails (non-YCSB schema)
3. Calls `parse_delete_from_relname(sql, relname, sizeof(relname))` to extract the table name from the `DELETE FROM <table>` clause; handles quoted identifiers (`"schema"."table"` form), schema-qualified names (`schema.table`), and bare names
4. Calls `RelnameGetRelid(relname)` to resolve the OID; returns early if invalid (table doesn't exist)
5. Computes `h = hash_any((unsigned char *) &keyval, sizeof(int32))`
6. Builds the `PREDICATELOCKTARGETTAG` as: `SET_PREDICATELOCKTARGETTAG_TUPLE(tag, 0, relOid, (BlockNumber)(h >> 16), (OffsetNumber)((h & 0xFFFF) | 1))` — this mirrors the tag an INSERT would create for that key
7. Calls `ws_table_reserveDT(&tag)` to reserve the tag in the write-set conflict table
8. Calls `store_optim_delete_by_key(relOid, keyval, GetCurrentCommandId(true))` to record the pending delete for deferred apply

**Why the hash-based tuple tag?** YCSB keys are inserted at heap positions determined
by their value hash.  Replicating that hash formula guarantees the DELETE-0 tag
matches the tag a concurrent INSERT would register, triggering a conflict check.

This ensures the conflict detection correctly handles the DELETE-before-INSERT
case in YCSB workloads.

#### `bcdb_worker_process_tx_dt()` — saveState/releaseState

After commit but before result formatting, scans `tx->sql` for "saveState" /
"releaseState" strings.  These are special recovery control transactions:

- `select saveState('snap_id')`: sets `block->snapTid = tx->tx_id` and waits
  on `condRecovery` with a 500ms timeout — pauses deterministic execution at
  this checkpoint.
- `select releaseState('snap_id')`: sets `block->snapTid = getpid()` and
  broadcasts `condRecovery` — unblocks a waiting saveState.

Used for recovery testing: creates a checkpoint where external processes can
inspect or modify database state before execution continues.

#### `bcdb_worker_process_tx_dt()` — PG_CATCH handler

On any error:
1. `ConditionVariableCancelSleep()` — cancel any pending CV wait
2. Explicit portal resource owner cleanup if `hold_portal_snapshot`
3. `bcdb_drain_optim_write_list(activeTx)` — drain without dropping slots
   (slots may be partially applied; `MemoryContextReset` reclaims memory)
4. `MemoryContextReset(bcdb_tx_context)`
5. `delete_tx(tx)` — remove from pool
6. `activeTx = NULL`
7. `PG_RE_THROW()` — propagate to PostgresMain's top-level handler

**`finish_xact_command()` is NOT called** in the error path.  Calling it on a
transaction in error state causes cascading warnings.  PostgresMain's `sigsetjmp`
handler calls `AbortCurrentTransaction()` which properly cleans up everything.

---

### shm_transaction.c

#### `rs_table_reserveDT()` / `ws_table_reserveDT()`

Both check `bcdb_dt_conflict_tracking` first and return immediately if off.
When on, allocate a `WSTableEntryRecord` from `bcdb_tx_context` and prepend to
the process-local list.  No shared memory touched.

This deferred-reservation design means:
- No shared-table write contention during execution
- Only committed write-sets are ever published (retried/conflicted txs never
  pollute the conflict tables)

`ws_table_reserve()` (the old eager-reservation variant) has been removed — all
call sites replaced by `ws_table_reserveDT()`.

#### `table_checkDT(PREDICATELOCKTARGETTAG *tag, WSTable *table)`

Core conflict predicate.  For each tag:

1. Probes `table->map` under per-partition spinlock (one of 512 partitions)
2. If `entry->tx_id < activeTx->tx_id` (earlier-ordered) AND
   `entry->tx_id > activeTx->tx_id_committed` (not in our snapshot baseline):
   conflict
3. If `pg_atomic_read_u32(&table->mapB_nonempty) == 0`: skip `mapB` probe
4. Otherwise probe `mapB` with the same logic

**The `mapB_nonempty` optimization**: Without this flag, every conflict check
would pay two hash lookups + spinlock acquires even when `mapB` is empty (the
common case between epoch switches).  The `pg_atomic_uint32` flag reduces the
second probe to a single cached-line atomic load in the common case.

#### `bcdb_xid_preceded_snapshot(BCTxID cand_id)` — ACTIVE

Called from `table_checkDT` after a candidate conflict entry passes the
`tx_id > tx_id_committed` check.  If the candidate's PostgreSQL XID
committed before the current tx's `snap_xmin`, the function returns `true`
and the conflict is skipped.  Full design in Section 9.

#### `ws_table_check()` (legacy non-DT path)

Returns false unconditionally because `ws_table_reserve()` (which populated
`ws_table->map`) has been removed.  The legacy conflict detection is a no-op.

#### `tx_queue_insert()`

Acquires `queue->lock`, waits on `full_cond` while `queue->size > QUEUEING_BLOCKS`,
enqueues at tail, releases, signals `empty_cond`.

Back-pressure: producer backends block when the per-queue depth exceeds
`QUEUEING_BLOCKS`.

#### `tx_queue_next(int worker_id)`

Dequeues from `tx_queues[worker_id % get_blksz()]`.  Blocks on `empty_cond`
when empty.  Signals `full_cond` after dequeue.  Records queue wait time for
the phase tracer via `bcdb_ptrace_note_queue_pop_wait`.

---

### middleware.c

#### `bcdb_middleware_submit_block_results(block_json)` — results submit path

Called by `bcdb_block_submit_results` in `func.c`.  Steps:
1. `++block_meta->global_bmax` — bump the global block counter
2. `parse_block_with_txs(block_json)` — allocates `BCDBShmXact` entries, assigns `tx_id`s sequentially, attaches to sentinel block 1
3. `tx_queue_insert(tx, tx->tx_id)` for each tx in order — enqueues for worker processing; `last_tx_id` is updated to the last tx's id
4. `bcdb_wait_until_committed(last_tx_id)` — waits for the contiguous global
   watermark to reach the last tx before reading the whole block's result set
5. Iterates all txs: `appendStringInfo` of `tx->hash`, `\t`, hex-encoded `block->result[slot]`, `\n`
6. Returns `out.data` — caller (`func.c`) wraps in text Datum

**Why the global watermark wait is required here:** `result_committed_txid[slot]`
is written at Step 10, before Step 11 serializes the final commit watermark.
So a later tx can publish its own slot before some earlier tx has reached its
final watermark update.  Waiting on `bcdb_wait_until_committed(last_tx_id)`
guarantees every tx `0..last_tx_id` has already completed Step 10 and Step 11,
which makes whole-block result assembly race-free.

#### `bcdb_middleware_submit_block(block_json)` — no-results submit path

Called by `bcdb_block_submit` in `func.c`.  Same enqueue path, then calls
`bcdb_wait_until_committed(last_tx_id)`.  Does not collect result strings —
returns a `char *` pointer to the last tx's result slot (`block->result[slot(last_tx_id)]`)
in shared memory.  The caller (`bcdb_block_submit`) discards this value and
returns `PG_RETURN_BOOL(true)`.

#### `bcdb_wait_until_committed(BCTxID target_tx_id)` — legacy global watermark wait

Polls `get_last_committed_txid(NULL)` until `>= target_tx_id`.  Uses 128-spin
backoff then 1→64 µs sleep doubling.  Always-on 5s watchdog logs
`[BCDB_HANG] committed_wait_stuck` if stuck.  This is the active wait used by
`bcdb_middleware_submit_block()` and `bcdb_middleware_submit_block_results()`
when they need the entire block's results to be safe to read.

#### `bcdb_wait_until_slot_ready(BCTxID target_tx_id)` — T3 per-slot wait

Loads `blk->result_committed_txid[slot]` with ACQUIRE ordering; breaks when
`published == target_tx_id`.  Same 128-spin + sleep backoff as above.  Hang
watchdog logs `[BCDB_HANG] slot_ready_stuck` with slot index, current slot value,
expected value, and elapsed time.  It remains useful for single-target polling
and debugging, but is intentionally not the block-level completion primitive.

---

### worker_controller.c

Manages worker process lifecycle.  Connects via libpq with startup parameters
`is_bcdb_worker=true`, `blocksize=N`, `worker_id=K`.  Monitors workers and
restarts them on crash.  The `'J'` protocol message triggers the infinite
dequeue loop in `postgres.c`.

---

*End of document — last updated 2026-04-21.*

---

## 17. Addendum: Function-Level Call Graphs and API Index (2026-04-20)

This addendum is **append-only**: it does not rewrite earlier sections; it
adds a function-by-function index and explicit “who calls what” mapping for
the DT hot path.

### 17.1 Deterministic Execution (DT) hot path — function-level call graph

The most common production path is:

1. A SQL caller invokes a SQL-exposed BCDB function (in `func.c`).
2. Middleware parses JSON and enqueues `BCDBShmXact`s (in `middleware.c`).
3. Worker backends dequeue and execute each tx deterministically (in `worker.c`).
4. Results are published to shared memory (sentinel block 1) for readback.

#### 17.1.1 SQL entrypoints → middleware

`src/backend/bcdb/func.c` defines SQL-callable functions (declared in
`src/include/bcdb/func.h`) that delegate into middleware:

- `Datum bcdb_init(PG_FUNCTION_ARGS)`
    - Calls `bcdb_middleware_init(is_oep_mode, block_size)`.
    - Typical caller: external client / extension SQL to bring the system up.

- `Datum bcdb_init2(PG_FUNCTION_ARGS)`
    - Calls `bcdb_middleware_init2(is_oep_mode, block_size, numTx, timeSlot)`.
    - Used for burst-based submit pacing in benchmarks.

- `Datum bcdb_block_submit(PG_FUNCTION_ARGS)`
    - Calls `bcdb_middleware_submit_block(block_json)`.
    - Returns `bool`; blocks until the **last tx’s slot** is ready.

- `Datum bcdb_block_submit_results(PG_FUNCTION_ARGS)`
    - Calls `bcdb_middleware_submit_block_results(block_json)`.
    - Returns a text payload containing one line per tx:
        `hash \t <hex-encoded-result> \n`.

- `Datum bcdb_tx_submit(PG_FUNCTION_ARGS)`
    - Calls `bcdb_middleware_submit_tx(tx_json)` (single-tx submit).

- `Datum bcdb_last_committed_txid(PG_FUNCTION_ARGS)`
    - Forces sentinel creation via `get_block_by_id(1, true)` then returns
        `get_last_committed_txid(NULL)`.

#### 17.1.2 Middleware submit → shared-memory tx objects → queue

`src/backend/bcdb/middleware.c` (declared in `src/include/bcdb/middleware.h`):

- `void bcdb_middleware_init(bool is_oep_mode, int32 block_size)`
    - Creates `bcdb_middleware_context`, ensures sentinel block 1 exists,
        sets `bcdb_worker_count` and `set_blksz()`, initializes the idle worker
        controller list.
    - Critical invariant enforced: if sentinel `block->blksize` already exists
        and differs from requested, middleware errors (restart required).

- `char *bcdb_middleware_submit_block(const char *block_json)`
    - `parse_block_with_txs()` creates a block + tx objects and assigns tx_id.
    - Enqueues each tx via `tx_queue_insert(tx, tx->tx_id)`.
    - Waits for the last tx via `bcdb_wait_until_committed(last_tx_id)`.
    - Returns `block->result[slot(last_tx_id)]`.

- `char *bcdb_middleware_submit_block_results(const char *block_json)`
    - Same enqueue as above, waits for last tx slot, then iterates all txs and
        assembles a tab-separated payload using `append_hex_encoded()`.
    - Uses the shared-memory result ring slots mapping via
        `bcdb_result_slot_for_txid(tx->tx_id)`.

- `BCDBShmXact *create_tx(...)` and `tx_queue_insert(...)` are the core
    primitives used here:
    - `create_tx()` is in `shm_transaction.c`.
    - `tx_queue_insert()` is in `shm_transaction.c`.

#### 17.1.3 Worker dequeue → optimistic run → gate → conflict/publish → apply → finish

The worker-side loop is driven from PostgreSQL integration (see §17.4), but
the deterministic tx body is `bcdb_worker_process_tx_dt()`:

`src/backend/bcdb/worker.c`:

1. `tx = tx_queue_next(worker_id)`
2. `bcdb_worker_process_tx_dt(tx, dualTab=true)`
     - `start_xact_command()`
     - `snapshot = GetTransactionSnapshot()` and `activeTx->snap_xmin = snapshot->xmin`
     - `get_write_set(tx, snapshot)` runs the SQL once under the fixed snapshot
         (SELECT results are captured via the modified `printtup.c`; see §17.4).
     - `bcdb_maybe_enqueue_deferred_delete0_by_key(tx)` reserves + buffers a
         deferred delete-by-key on the YCSB “DELETE 0” case.
     - **Lever D v2 serial gate**: `bcdb_wait_for_serial_slot(tx, block1)`
     - `rw_conflicts = conflict_checkDT()` (only does work when
         `bcdb_dt_conflict_tracking=on`)
     - `publish_ws_tableDT(tx->tx_id)` publishes the tx’s write-set into the DT
         conflict map for future txs.
     - `set_published_max_txid(tx)` releases the next tx past the gate.
     - Apply phase: `bcdb_apply_optim_writes_with_retry(tx, ...)` ultimately
         calls `apply_optim_writes()` (in `shm_transaction.c`).
     - Finish phase:
         - Formats `block1->result[slot]`.
         - Publishes per-slot commit markers:
             - `__atomic_store_n(&block->result_commit_xid[slot], tx->xid, RELEASE)`
             - `__atomic_store_n(&block->result_committed_txid[slot], tx_id, RELEASE)`
         - Serializes commit watermark:
             - `bcdb_wait_for_prev_committed(tx)`
             - `set_last_committed_txid(tx)`
         - `delete_tx(tx)` and `MemoryContextReset(bcdb_tx_context)`.

### 17.2 API index — public functions and how they are used

This section enumerates functions that are **extern** in `src/include/bcdb/*`
and explains their role + primary call sites. (Static helpers inside `.c`
files are covered only when they are hot-path critical.)

#### 17.2.1 `globals.h` / `globals.c`

- Global mode flags and runtime knobs:
    - `is_bcdb_master`, `is_bcdb_worker`
    - `OEP_mode`, `bcdb_worker_count`
    - `bcdb_dt_conflict_tracking`, `bcdb_serial_gate_mode`
    - `bcdb_dt_hashtab_switch_threshold`, `bcdb_result_ring_slots`
    - Used throughout BCDB modules and some PostgreSQL core files to choose BCDB
        code paths.

- `uint64 bcdb_get_time(void)`
    - Microsecond wall-clock helper used for watchdogs, timing, phase tracer.

- `int Base64Encode(const unsigned char *buffer, size_t length, char **b64text)`
    - Used by crypto / signature helpers (and potentially client payload code).

#### 17.2.2 `shm_block.h` / `shm_block.c`

- `Size block_pool_size(void)`
    - Called from PostgreSQL shared-memory sizing during startup.

- `void create_block_pool(void)`
    - Called once at postmaster start (via PostgreSQL SHM init path).
    - Initializes `block_meta`, `block_pool_lock`, and the shared hash table.

- `BCBlock *get_block_by_id(BCBlockID id, bool create_if_not_found)`
    - Central block accessor. Used by middleware, workers, printtup capture,
        and SQL helper functions.
    - Creates sentinel block 1 lazily and seeds its `blksize`.

- Block membership / lifecycle:
    - `void block_add_tx(BCBlock *block, BCDBShmXact *tx)`
        - Used by middleware paths that attach existing tx objects to a block.
    - `void delete_block(BCBlock *block)`
        - Used by `block_cleaning()` after per-tx `delete_tx()`.

- Legacy per-block commit helpers:
    - `int get_commited(int id)` / `void set_commited(int id, BCDBShmXact *tx)`
        - Historical helpers used by older non-T3 completion tracking.
        - Most DT paths now use `result_committed_txid[slot]` + `last_committed_tx_id`.

- `BCBlock *bcdb_get_block1(void)`
    - Convenience accessor for sentinel block 1.

- `void set_last_committed_txid(BCDBShmXact *tx)` / `BCTxID get_last_committed_txid(BCDBShmXact *tx)`
    - Global commit watermark accessors. Worker finish path calls setter.
    - Middleware’s legacy wait helper polls the getter.

- `void advance_last_committed_txid(BCDBShmXact *tx)`
    - Infrastructure: lock-free CAS prefix-scan over T3 per-slot markers.
    - Currently **implemented but not called** in the worker DT finish path.

- Lever D v2 gate counters:
    - `void set_published_max_txid(BCDBShmXact *tx)`
    - `BCTxID get_published_max_txid(BCDBShmXact *tx)`
    - Used by `bcdb_wait_for_serial_slot()` in `worker.c`.

- Worker count / runtime sizing:
    - `void set_blksz(int num)` / `BCTxID get_blksz(void)`
    - `int bcdb_get_result_ring_slots(void)` / `int bcdb_get_runtime_result_ring_slots(void)`
    - Used by middleware, workers, and printtup result capture.

- Submission counters:
    - `void set_num_tx_sub(int num)` / `BCTxID get_num_tx_sub(void)`
    - `void set_num_txqd(int num)` / `BCTxID get_num_txqd(void)`
    - Used as global counters (e.g., `parse_block_with_txs()` uses `num_tx_sub`
        to allocate a contiguous tx_id range).

#### 17.2.3 `shm_transaction.h` / `shm_transaction.c`

- Shared-memory init:
    - `void create_tx_pool(void)` / `Size tx_pool_size(void)` / `void clear_tx_pool(void)`
    - Called at postmaster startup and from reset paths.

- Tx object lifecycle:
    - `BCDBShmXact *create_tx(char *hash, char *sql, BCTxID tx_id, BCBlockID snapshot_block, int isolation, bool pred_lock)`
        - Called from middleware JSON parsing (`parse_tx`, `parse_block_with_txs`)
            and from direct deterministic execution paths in `postgres.c`.
    - `void delete_tx(BCDBShmXact *tx)`
        - Called after completion or cleanup (worker finish, block cleaning).
        - Two-phase: calls `remove_tx_xid_map(tx->xid)` first (LWLock scope), then removes the entry from `tx_pool` under `tx_pool_lock`.
    - `BCDBShmXact *get_tx_by_hash(const char *hash)`
        - Used by middleware wait and “is committed?” helpers.

- XID map:
    - `void add_tx_xid_map(TransactionId id, BCDBShmXact *tx)`
    - `void remove_tx_xid_map(TransactionId id)`
    - Used to map PostgreSQL `xid` → BCDB tx for cleanup and debugging.

- Queueing:
    - `void tx_queue_insert(BCDBShmXact *tx, int32 partition)`
        - Called by middleware producers to enqueue txs.
        - Back-pressure: blocks on `queue->full_cond` while queue size exceeds
            `QUEUEING_BLOCKS`.
        - Signals `queue->empty_cond` after enqueue.
    - `BCDBShmXact *tx_queue_next(int32 partition)`
        - Called by worker consumers to dequeue.
        - Blocks on `queue->empty_cond` while empty.
        - Signals `queue->full_cond` after dequeue.

- DT conflict tracking lists (process-local during execution):
    - `void ws_table_reserveDT(PREDICATELOCKTARGETTAG *tag)`
        - Called from `nodeModifyTable.c` DML hooks and retry paths.
    - `void rs_table_reserveDT(const PREDICATELOCKTARGETTAG *tag)`
        - Called from PostgreSQL SSI predicate lock acquisition (`predicate.c`).

- DT conflict check / publish:
    - `int conflict_checkDT(void)`
        - Called from `worker.c` after passing the Lever D gate.
    - `void publish_ws_tableDT(int id)`
        - Called from `worker.c` after a clean conflict check.
        - Note: it records the **latest** tx_id seen for a tag in the active shard
            (tx_ids publish in order, so “latest preceding writer” is sufficient).

- Hashing / legacy maintenance:
    - `uint32 dummy_hash(const void *key, Size key_size)`
        - Identity hash used by the partitioned WS/RS hash tables.
        - BCDB already computes a `uint32` hash for `PREDICATELOCKTARGETTAG` via
            `PredicateLockTargetTagHashCode()`. Using that precomputed value as the
            key plus `dummy_hash()` avoids double-hashing.
    - `void clean_rs_ws_table(void)`
        - Bulk-clears the **legacy non-DT** ws/rs maps (`ws_table->map` and
            `rs_table->map`).
        - Called at block boundary in the non-DT worker path (see `worker.c`).

- Optimistic write buffering (execution-time):
    - `store_optim_insert`, `store_optim_update`, `store_optim_delete`
        - Called from DML interception in `nodeModifyTable.c` while executing.
    - `store_optim_delete_by_key`
        - Called from `bcdb_maybe_enqueue_deferred_delete0_by_key()` to represent
            a DELETE-0 as a deferred “delete by primary key” operation.

- Apply phase:
    - `bool apply_optim_writes(void)`
        - Called by `bcdb_apply_optim_writes_with_retry()`; walks
            `activeTx->optim_write_list` and executes buffered heap/index mutations.
    - `bool apply_optim_insert(TupleTableSlot *slot, CommandId cid)`
    - `bool apply_optim_update(ItemPointer tid, TupleTableSlot *slot, CommandId cid)`
    - `bool apply_optim_delete(Oid relOid, ItemPointer tupleid, TupleTableSlot *storedSlot, CommandId cid)`
        - Each runs inside subtransaction-aware error handling; these return `false`
            for retryable outcomes (e.g., duplicate key on insert) so Lever D v2 can
            retry apply.
    - `bool apply_deferred_delete_by_key(Oid relOid, int keyval)`
        - Apply-side implementation of the deferred “delete by primary key” operation.
    - `void bcdb_reset_apply_error_flags(void)` / `bool bcdb_apply_had_unique_violation(void)`
        - Used to classify errors as retryable vs terminal-noop.

- Merkle update batching:
    - `merkle_record_update` / `apply_merkle_changeset`
        - Used during apply to aggregate Merkle XOR updates and apply as a batch.

#### 17.2.4 `worker.h` / `worker.c`

- `bool bcdb_worker_init(void)`
    - Called from `get_block_by_id()` as a “make sure worker contexts exist”
        hook, and from worker startup paths.

- `void bcdb_worker_process_tx(BCDBShmXact *tx)` (legacy non-DT path)
    - Older execution path used when DT is not active. The current production
        deterministic path is `bcdb_worker_process_tx_dt()`.

- `void bcdb_worker_process_tx_dt(BCDBShmXact *tx, bool dual_table)`
    - DT transaction body (the core deterministic execution runtime).

- `void safedb_txdt(BCDBShmXact *tx, bool dualTab)` (internal helper)
    - Wrapper that restores `is_bcdb_worker` even on error; used by
        direct “single tx” execution paths.

- `void bcdb_on_worker_exit(int code, Datum arg)`
    - Worker exit hook registered to clean up BCDB worker state.

- `BCBlockID GetCurrentTxBlockId(void)` / `BCBlockID GetCurrentTxBlockIdSnapshot(void)`
    - Helpers for introspection / debugging.

- Phase tracing (`bcdb_ptrace_*`)
    - Lightweight per-transaction timing + counter accounting.
    - Enabled by setting `BCDB_PHASE_TRACE=<path-prefix>` in the worker process
        environment; each backend appends CSV rows to `<prefix>.<pid>`.
    - Exported API used by both `worker.c` and `shm_transaction.c`:
        `bcdb_ptrace_enabled()`, `bcdb_ptrace_now_us()`, `bcdb_ptrace_add_us()`,
        `bcdb_ptrace_inc_counter()`, plus the inline wrappers
        `bcdb_ptrace_timer_start()` / `bcdb_ptrace_timer_stop()`.

#### 17.2.5 `middleware.h` / `middleware.c`

- Init:
    - `bcdb_middleware_init`, `bcdb_middleware_init2`

- Submit:
    - `bcdb_middleware_submit_tx`
    - `bcdb_middleware_submit_block`
    - `bcdb_middleware_submit_block_results`
    - `bcdb_middleware_submit_block2` (burst mode)

- Block/tx bookkeeping:
    - `bcdb_middleware_set_txs_committed_block(tx_hash, block_id)`
        - Looks up tx by hash, looks up/creates the target block, then calls
            `bcdb_middleware_attach_tx_to_block()` → `block_add_tx()`.

- Lifecycle / maintenance:
    - `bcdb_wait_tx_finish`, `bcdb_is_tx_commited`, `bcdb_middleware_wait_all_to_finish`
    - `block_cleaning` (also called from SQL status helpers / benchmark loops)
    - `bcdb_clear_block_txs_store` (reset: clears blocks, clears tx pool,
        disconnects idle workers)

- Legacy gating / conflict hooks:
    - `bcdb_middleware_allow_txs_exec_write_set_and_commit(BCBlock *block)` /
        `allow_all_block_txs_to_commit(BCBlock *block)`
        - Currently `allow_all_block_txs_to_commit()` is a stub (returns
            immediately), so commit gating is effectively disabled.
    - `bcdb_middleware_conflict_check(BCBlock *block)`
        - Currently a stub (returns immediately). Historical placeholder for a
            middleware-level conflict graph check.

#### 17.2.6 `func.h` / `func.c` (SQL-callable surface)

These are the SQL entrypoints exposed to clients (and used by benchmarks):

- `bcdb_init(is_oep_mode bool, block_size int)` / `bcdb_init2(is_oep_mode bool, block_size int, numTx int, timeSlot int)`
    - Initialize middleware + worker controller and seed sentinel sizing.

- `bcdb_block_submit(block_json cstring)` / `bcdb_block_submit_results(block_json cstring)`
    - Submit a JSON block (array of tx objects with `hash` and `sql`).

- `bcdb_tx_submit(tx_json cstring)`
    - Submit a single tx.

- `bcdb_add_tx_with_block_id(tx_hash cstring, block_id int)`
    - Manually attach a tx (by hash) to a block id.

- `bcdb_allow_txs_commit_by_block_id(block_id int)`
    - Calls `bcdb_middleware_allow_txs_exec_write_set_and_commit_by_id()`.
    - In current code this is effectively a no-op gate (see `allow_all_block_txs_to_commit`).

- `bcdb_check_txs_result(tx_hash cstring)`
    - `bcdb_wait_tx_finish(tx_hash)` then `bcdb_is_tx_commited(tx_hash)`.

- `bcdb_num_committed()` / `bcdb_last_committed_txid()`
    - Introspection helpers for benchmarking / diagnostics.

- `bcdb_reset()`
    - Calls `bcdb_clear_block_txs_store()`.

- `bcdb_check_block_status()` / `bcdb_wait_to_finish()`
    - Debug helpers (status logging / sleeps).

- `bcdb_verify(publicKey text, plainText text, signature text)`
    - RSA signature verification helper (OpenSSL-based).
    - `publicKey` must be **PEM-formatted** (loaded via `PEM_read_bio_RSA_PUBKEY`).
    - This is **not** the same path as the `postgres.c` inline verifier, which
      expects a Base64-encoded DER SubjectPublicKeyInfo key and uses `d2i_PUBKEY`
      with a process-local key+context cache and telemetry.

#### 17.2.7 `worker_controller.h` / `worker_controller.c`

This module manages libpq connections to dedicated worker backends.

- Idle list:
    - `idle_worker_list_init`, `get_idle_worker`, `add_idle_worker`

- Worker connection lifecycle:
    - `create_worker_controller`, `worker_start`, `worker_execute`,
        `worker_restart`, `worker_finish`

Workers are started with `-c is_bcdb_worker=true` and are driven by the
PostgreSQL core “J protocol” loop (see §17.4).

The controller connects using `bcdb_host` / `bcdb_port`, which are populated in
`postmaster.c` by parsing the startup packet fields `host` and `port`.

#### 17.2.8 `bcdb_dsa.h` / `bcdb_dsa.c`

- `Size bcdb_dsa_shm_size(void)` / `void create_bcdb_dsa(void)` /
    `void attach_bcdb_dsa(void)`
    - Infrastructure for a BCDB-owned DSA area. Not central to the DT hot path.

#### 17.2.9 `blockprocessor.h` and `bcdb/utils/*.h` (support libraries)

These headers exist in `src/include/bcdb/` but are not part of the DT
deterministic execution contract itself.

- `src/include/bcdb/blockprocessor.h`
    - Currently a placeholder (header guard only). No exported API.

- `src/include/bcdb/utils/*.h`
    - Generic support libraries used by BCDB code:
        - `aligned_heap.h` — alignment-aware allocator used by shared-memory
            structures (included by `shm_transaction.c`).
        - `concurrent_queue.h`, `concurrent_list.h` — concurrency-friendly
            containers (used by BCDB utilities and tests).
        - `st_list.h`, `st_queue.h`, `sys-queue.h` — simple list/queue helpers.
        - `sysinfo.h` — platform/system helpers (currently appears unused in
            mainline BCDB code).
        - `cJSON.h` — vendored JSON library API. BCDB uses it internally (e.g.,
            worker-side JSON parsing helpers) but the full cJSON surface is not
            BCDB-specific.

### 17.3 Notes and clarifications (to avoid common misreads)

- `publish_ws_tableDT()` stores the **latest** preceding writer tx_id for a tag,
    not the earliest. Because DT publish happens in tx_id order (enforced by the
    gate), the latest preceding writer is sufficient for conflict detection.

- Queue condition variables:
    - `tx_queue_insert()` blocks on `full_cond` when the queue is too large, and
        signals `empty_cond` after enqueue.
    - `tx_queue_next()` blocks on `empty_cond` when the queue is empty, and
        signals `full_cond` after dequeue.

- `bcdb_xid_preceded_snapshot()` is **ACTIVE** — called in `table_checkDT()`
    for both the `map` and `mapB` shard probes.  When a candidate entry satisfies
    `tx_id > tx_id_committed`, the function checks whether the candidate's XID
    preceded the current tx's `snap_xmin`; if so the conflict is skipped and
    execution continues without retrying.

### 17.4 PostgreSQL hook points (non-BCDB files) and how they call BCDB

BCDB relies on modifications to a few PostgreSQL core modules. The key
integration points are:

- `src/backend/tcop/postgres.c`
    - Implements a `'J'` protocol path used by dedicated BCDB worker backends
        to enter an infinite dequeue/execute loop.
    - Also provides a direct deterministic execution debug path (`s <txid> <sql>`)
        that creates a `BCDBShmXact` and calls `safedb_txdt()`.
    - Contains a full EVP-based signing infrastructure (`base64_decode`,
        `verify_signature_b64key`, process-local `EVP_PKEY`/`EVP_MD_CTX` cache,
        `BcdbSigVerifyMetrics` telemetry, `bcdb_log_signature_verify_summary`,
        `bcdb_client_public_key_or_default`, `bcdb_handle_signature_failure`)
        used by the simple-query path to check `##`-delimited signatures before
        dispatching to `safedb_txdt()` / `exec_simple_query()`.
    - The public key is **GUC-configurable** per session (`bcdb_client_public_key`);
        when the GUC is empty, a hardcoded Base64-DER development key is used as a
        fallback (not the only source).  Key format is Base64-DER (`d2i_PUBKEY`),
        not PEM.
    - Signature verification failures are conditionally enforced: when
        `bcdb_enforce_signatures = on`, an `ERROR` is raised and the statement is
        rejected; when `off`, a `WARNING` is emitted and execution continues.

- `src/backend/executor/nodeModifyTable.c`
    - Intercepts INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE during BCDB worker execution:
        - Reserves DT write-set tags via `ws_table_reserveDT()`.
        - Buffers writes via `store_optim_insert/update/delete()`.
    - Adds BCDB-specific helpers used by that interception:
        - `bcdb_compute_key_tag()` to tag operations by primary-key value.
        - `bcdb_activeTx_update()` to reconcile BCDB buffered writes with the
            executor’s update path.

- `src/backend/storage/lmgr/predicate.c`
    - Hooks SSI predicate locking for BCDB workers and appends read-set tags via
        `rs_table_reserveDT()`.
    - Disables PostgreSQL’s normal SSI “conflict-out” checks for BCDB workers by
        short-circuiting `CheckForSerializableConflictOut()` when
        `is_bcdb_worker=true` (BCDB relies on DT conflict detection instead).

- `src/backend/access/common/printtup.c`
    - When `is_bcdb_worker=true`, captures SELECT output rows into a transient
        `rowStr` buffer and copies it into `block1->result[slot]`.
    - `bcdb_worker_process_tx_dt()` reads this value at the serial gate and
        later republishes it (after commit) with T3 markers.

Additional PostgreSQL-core touchpoints that are BCDB-significant:

- `src/backend/storage/ipc/ipci.c`
    - Sizes and initializes BCDB shared-memory pools during postmaster startup:
        - Adds `block_pool_size()` and `tx_pool_size()` to the SHM size budget.
        - Calls `create_tx_pool()` and `create_block_pool()` during SHM init.

- `src/backend/postmaster/postmaster.c`
    - Extends the startup packet parsing with BCDB-specific parameters:
        - Captures `host` and `port` fields into `bcdb_host` / `bcdb_port`.

- `src/backend/utils/misc/guc.c`
    - Declares BCDB mode flags and runtime knobs as GUCs (e.g.,
        `is_bcdb_master`, `is_bcdb_worker`, `bcdb_worker_count`,
        `bcdb_dt_conflict_tracking`, `bcdb_serial_gate_mode`,
        `bcdb_dt_hashtab_switch_threshold`, `bcdb_result_ring_slots`,
        `OEP_mode`, `blocksize`, `worker_id`).

- `src/backend/tcop/pquery.c`
    - Alters portal strategy and per-portal memory cleanup when
        `is_bcdb_worker=true` (forces `PORTAL_MULTI_QUERY` for the worker path
        and skips `MemoryContextDeleteChildren()` in the multi-statement loop).

- `src/backend/utils/time/snapmgr.c`
    - In `GetTransactionSnapshot()`, BCDB workers always take a
        serializable-style transaction snapshot (`GetSerializableTransactionSnapshot`)
        and register/copy it as the first xact snapshot.

- `src/backend/utils/error/elog.c`
    - Suppresses sending error messages to the frontend when
        `is_bcdb_worker=true` (workers are driven by middleware, not direct
        clients).

- `src/backend/access/heap/heapam_visibility.c`
    - In `HeapTupleSatisfiesVisibility(SNAPSHOT_MVCC)`, BCDB workers route
        non-catalog tuple visibility checks through `BCDBHeapTupleSatisfiesBlock()`.

- `src/backend/access/transam/subtrans.c`
    - Relaxes TransactionXmin-based assertions when `is_bcdb_worker=true`,
        allowing subtransaction parent lookups on older XIDs.

- `src/backend/access/nbtree/nbtinsert.c`
    - During unique-check duplicate detection, BCDB workers request a
        `conflict_slot` from `table_index_fetch_tuple_check()` (enables BCDB to
        inspect the conflicting heap tuple during deterministic execution).

- `src/backend/access/merkle/merklebuild.c`, `src/backend/access/merkle/merkleutil.c`
    - Temporarily override `is_bcdb_worker=false` around certain Merkle
        maintenance paths (so background/maintenance work isn’t affected by
        BCDB worker-only behavior like protocol output suppression).

- `src/backend/access/heap/pruneheap.c`
    - Minor pruning-hint behavior change for BCDB workers when tuple raw xmax is
        invalid.

- `src/backend/access/heap/heapam_handler.c`
    - Contains BCDB-worker-only scan loops that repeatedly invoke
        `CheckForSerializableConflictOut()` in some non-index heap scan paths.
        In current code this is effectively a no-op for BCDB workers because
        `predicate.c` short-circuits `CheckForSerializableConflictOut()` when
        `is_bcdb_worker=true`.

- `src/backend/access/transam/xact.c`
    - Contains commented-out BCDB worker checks near transaction commit paths
        (appears to be experimental/incomplete rather than an active hook).

Other files include BCDB headers but currently appear to have no substantive
BCDB-specific logic beyond includes (potentially dead includes or future hooks),
for example: `commands/trigger.c`, `utils/mmgr/aset.c`, `utils/hash/dynahash.c`,
`executor/execIndexing.c`, `storage/buffer/bufmgr.c`, `storage/page/bufpage.c`,
`storage/page/itemptr.c`, `storage/ipc/shmqueue.c`, `access/heap/heapam.c`.

